  {"id":"cuijpers2013","authors":["Cuijpers P","Berking M","Andersson G","Quigley L","Kleiboer A","Dobson KS"],"year":2013,"title":"A Meta-Analysis of Cognitive-Behavioural Therapy for Adult Depression, Alone and in Comparison with other Treatments","journal":"The Canadian Journal of Psychiatry","volume":"58","issue":"7","pages":"376–385","doi":"10.1177/070674371305800702","pmid":"23870719","type":"Meta-analysis","n":"115 studies","effect":"g = 0.71 (0.53 bias-adjusted)","areas":["depression"],"finding":"CBT vs control gave g = 0.71 (NNT 2.6), but g = 0.53 after adjusting for publication bias and in higher-quality studies.","simple":"CBT clearly helps depression; good-quality studies suggest a moderate effect.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"watkins2012","authors":["Watkins ER","Taylor RS","Byng R","Baeyens C","Read R","Pearson K","Watson L"],"year":2012,"title":"Guided self-help concreteness training as an intervention for major depression in primary care: a Phase II randomized controlled trial","journal":"Psychological Medicine","volume":"42","issue":"7","pages":"1359–1371","doi":"10.1017/s0033291711002480","pmid":"22085757","type":"Phase II RCT","n":"Primary-care patients with major depression","effect":"4.3 points on HAM-D vs usual care","areas":["depression"],"finding":"Guided self-help \"concreteness training\" added to usual care improved depression (4.28 points on the Hamilton scale) at post-treatment and at 3 and 6 months, and reduced rumination and overgeneralisation.","simple":"Practising thinking in specific, concrete terms reduced depression in GP patients.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"webb2012","authors":["Webb TL","Miles E","Sheeran P"],"year":2012,"title":"Dealing with feeling: A meta-analysis of the effectiveness of strategies derived from the process model of emotion regulation","journal":"Psychological Bulletin","volume":"138","issue":"4","pages":"775–808","doi":"10.1037/a0027600","pmid":"22582737","type":"Meta-analysis","n":"306 experimental comparisons","effect":"Reappraisal d = 0.36","areas":["depression","anxiety","impulsiveness"],"finding":"Reappraising the emotional situation (d = 0.36) and taking another perspective (d = 0.45) changed emotions, whereas suppressing thoughts did not help.","simple":"Changing how you look at a situation changes how you feel; pushing thoughts away does not.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"lejuez2011","authors":["Lejuez CW","Hopko DR","Acierno R","Daughters SB","Pagoto SL"],"year":2011,"title":"Ten Year Revision of the Brief Behavioral Activation Treatment for Depression: Revised Treatment Manual","journal":"Behavior Modification","volume":"35","issue":"2","pages":"111–161","doi":"10.1177/0145445510390929","pmid":"21324944","type":"Treatment manual","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["depression","self-discipline"],"finding":"The revised Brief Behavioral Activation Treatment for Depression (BATD-R): identify values across life areas, choose activities that serve those values, then schedule, monitor and gradually build them up.","simple":"Pick activities that matter to you, put them in your diary, and track how they affect your mood.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"watkins2011","authors":["Watkins ER","Mullan E","Wingrove J","Rimes K","Steiner H","Bathurst N","Eastman R","Scott J"],"year":2011,"title":"Rumination-focused cognitive–behavioural therapy for residual depression: phase II randomised controlled trial","journal":"British Journal of Psychiatry","volume":"199","issue":"4","pages":"317–322","doi":"10.1192/bjp.bp.110.090282","pmid":"21778171","type":"Phase II RCT","n":"Adults with residual depression","effect":"","areas":["depression"],"finding":"Adding rumination-focused CBT to usual care significantly improved residual symptoms and remission rates, and the benefit was explained by reduced rumination.","simple":"Learning to catch and redirect rumination helps people whose depression lingers.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"bell2009","authors":["Bell AC","D'Zurilla TJ"],"year":2009,"title":"Problem-solving therapy for depression: A meta-analysis","journal":"Clinical Psychology Review","volume":"29","issue":"4","pages":"348–353","doi":"10.1016/j.cpr.2009.02.003","pmid":"19299058","type":"Meta-analysis","n":"21 independent samples","effect":"","areas":["depression"],"finding":"Problem-solving therapy was as effective as other psychotherapies and medication and better than no treatment. It worked best when it trained a positive problem orientation plus all four skills: defining the problem, generating options, deciding, and implementing/checking.","simple":"Learning a step-by-step way to tackle problems reduces depression.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"watkins2008","authors":["Watkins ER"],"year":2008,"title":"Constructive and unconstructive repetitive thought","journal":"Psychological Bulletin","volume":"134","issue":"2","pages":"163–206","doi":"10.1037/0033-2909.134.2.163","pmid":"18298268","type":"Theoretical review","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["depression","anxiety"],"finding":"Repetitive thinking can be helpful or harmful. Abstract, evaluative \"Why is this happening to me?\" thinking keeps distress going, whereas concrete, specific \"How can I…?\" thinking supports problem solving.","simple":"Going round in circles asking \"why?\" makes things worse; asking \"how?\" moves you forward.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"nolenhoeksema2008","authors":["Nolen-Hoeksema S","Wisco BE","Lyubomirsky S"],"year":2008,"title":"Rethinking Rumination","journal":"Perspectives on Psychological Science","volume":"3","issue":"5","pages":"400–424","doi":"10.1111/j.1745-6924.2008.00088.x","pmid":"26158958","type":"Review","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["depression","anxiety"],"finding":"Rumination — repetitively dwelling on the causes and consequences of distress — predicts the onset and length of depressive episodes, worsens negative thinking and impairs problem solving.","simple":"Dwelling on how bad you feel keeps depression going longer.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"cuijpers2007ba","authors":["Cuijpers P","van Straten A","Warmerdam L"],"year":2007,"title":"Behavioral activation treatments of depression: A meta-analysis","journal":"Clinical Psychology Review","volume":"27","issue":"3","pages":"318–326","doi":"10.1016/j.cpr.2006.11.001","pmid":"17184887","type":"Meta-analysis","n":"16 studies, 780 participants","effect":"d = 0.87 vs control","areas":["depression"],"finding":"Activity scheduling produced a large effect compared with control conditions (0.87) and was as effective as cognitive therapy (difference 0.02); benefits were maintained at follow-up.","simple":"Planning enjoyable and meaningful activities lifts mood about as much as full cognitive therapy.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"dimidjian2006","authors":["Dimidjian S","Hollon SD","Dobson KS","Schmaling KB","Kohlenberg RJ","Addis ME","Gallop R","McGlinchey JB","Markley DK","Gollan JK","Atkins DC","Dunner DL","Jacobson NS"],"year":2006,"title":"Randomized trial of behavioral activation, cognitive therapy, and antidepressant medication in the acute treatment of adults with major depression","journal":"Journal of Consulting and Clinical Psychology","volume":"74","issue":"4","pages":"658–670","doi":"10.1037/0022-006x.74.4.658","pmid":"16881773","type":"Randomized controlled trial","n":"241 adults with major depression","effect":"","areas":["depression"],"finding":"Among more severely depressed patients, behavioural activation was as effective as antidepressant medication, and both outperformed cognitive therapy.","simple":"Simply re-engaging with meaningful activities worked as well as medication for more severe depression.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"seligman2005","authors":["Seligman MEP","Steen TA","Park N","Peterson C"],"year":2005,"title":"Positive Psychology Progress: Empirical Validation of Interventions","journal":"American Psychologist","volume":"60","issue":"5","pages":"410–421","doi":"10.1037/0003-066x.60.5.410","pmid":"16045394","type":"Randomized placebo-controlled trial (online)","n":"6-arm trial","effect":"","areas":["depression"],"finding":"Of five happiness exercises tested against a placebo exercise, three — including \"three good things\" and using signature strengths in new ways — lastingly increased happiness and decreased depressive symptoms.","simple":"Writing down three good things each day lifted mood for months.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"kroenke2001","authors":["Kroenke K","Spitzer RL","Williams JBW"],"year":2001,"title":"The PHQ-9: Validity of a brief depression severity measure","journal":"Journal of General Internal Medicine","volume":"16","issue":"9","pages":"606–613","doi":"10.1046/j.1525-1497.2001.016009606.x","pmid":"11556941","type":"Validation study","n":"6,000 primary-care and obstetrics patients; 580 interviewed","effect":"Sensitivity 88%, specificity 88% at ≥10","areas":["depression","general"],"finding":"The 9-item PHQ-9 is a reliable, valid measure of depression severity. A score of 10 or more had 88% sensitivity and 88% specificity for major depression; scores of 5, 10, 15 and 20 mark mild, moderate, moderately severe and severe depression.","simple":"The PHQ-9 is a short, well-tested questionnaire for tracking low mood over time.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"martell2001","authors":["Martell CR","Addis ME","Jacobson NS"],"year":2001,"title":"Depression in Context: Strategies for Guided Action","publisher":"W. W. Norton","type":"Book / treatment manual","areas":["depression"],"finding":"Modern behavioural activation: depression is maintained by avoidance (\"TRAP\": Trigger, Response, Avoidance Pattern) and treated by guided action (\"TRAC\": Trigger, Response, Alternative Coping).","simple":"Notice what you avoid when low, and choose an alternative action instead.","journal":"","volume":"","issue":"","pages":"","doi":"","pmid":"","n":"—","effect":"","verified":"Bibliographic record"},
  {"id":"jacobson1996","authors":["Jacobson NS","Dobson KS","Truax PA","Addis ME","Koerner K","Gollan JK","Gortner E","Prince SE"],"year":1996,"title":"A component analysis of cognitive-behavioral treatment for depression","journal":"Journal of Consulting and Clinical Psychology","volume":"64","issue":"2","pages":"295–304","doi":"10.1037/0022-006x.64.2.295","pmid":"8871414","type":"Component RCT","n":"150 outpatients","effect":"","areas":["depression"],"finding":"Behavioural activation on its own did as well as full cognitive therapy at the end of treatment and at 6-month follow-up — the finding that launched BA as a stand-alone treatment.","simple":"The \"doing\" part of CBT alone was as powerful as the full package.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"jamison1995","authors":["Jamison C","Scogin F"],"year":1995,"title":"The outcome of cognitive bibliotherapy with depressed adults","journal":"Journal of Consulting and Clinical Psychology","volume":"63","issue":"4","pages":"644–650","doi":"10.1037/0022-006x.63.4.644","pmid":"7673542","type":"Randomized controlled trial","n":"80 depressed adults","effect":"","areas":["depression","general"],"finding":"Minimal-contact cognitive bibliotherapy (reading a CBT self-help book) beat a waiting list, with statistically and clinically significant gains maintained at 3 months and fewer negative automatic thoughts.","simple":"Working through a CBT self-help book reduced depression.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"burns1980","authors":["Burns DD"],"year":1980,"title":"Feeling Good: The New Mood Therapy","publisher":"William Morrow","type":"Self-help book","areas":["depression","general"],"finding":"Popularised the list of ten cognitive distortions and the daily mood log; tested as bibliotherapy in randomised trials (e.g., Jamison & Scogin, 1995).","simple":"A classic CBT self-help book that has been tested in trials.","journal":"","volume":"","issue":"","pages":"","doi":"","pmid":"","n":"—","effect":"","verified":"Bibliographic record"},
  {"id":"beck1979","authors":["Beck AT","Rush AJ","Shaw BF","Emery G"],"year":1979,"title":"Cognitive Therapy of Depression","publisher":"Guilford Press","type":"Book / treatment manual","areas":["depression","general"],"finding":"The founding manual of cognitive therapy: depression is maintained by negative automatic thoughts about the self, world and future (the \"cognitive triad\"), which can be identified, tested and changed.","simple":"How we interpret events shapes how we feel — and those interpretations can be tested.","journal":"","volume":"","issue":"","pages":"","doi":"","pmid":"","n":"—","effect":"","verified":"Bibliographic record"},
  {"id":"who-anxiety","authors":["World Health Organization"],"year":2025,"title":"Anxiety disorders — fact sheet","publisher":"WHO","type":"Fact sheet","url":"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/anxiety-disorders","areas":["anxiety"],"finding":"In 2021 an estimated 359 million people (4.4% of the global population) had an anxiety disorder, the most common of all mental disorders; psychological treatments such as CBT are effective.","simple":"Anxiety disorders are the most common mental health conditions in the world.","journal":"","volume":"","issue":"","pages":"","doi":"","pmid":"","n":"—","effect":"","verified":"Official source"},
  {"id":"balban2023","authors":["Balban MY","Neri E","Kogon MM","Weed L","Nouriani B","Jo B","Holl G","Zeitzer JM","Spiegel D","Huberman AD"],"year":2023,"title":"Brief structured respiration practices enhance mood and reduce physiological arousal","journal":"Cell Reports Medicine","volume":"4","issue":"1","pages":"100895","doi":"10.1016/j.xcrm.2022.100895","pmid":"36630953","type":"Randomized controlled trial","n":"Healthy adults, 1 month","effect":"","areas":["anxiety"],"finding":"Five minutes a day of breathwork — especially exhale-focused \"cyclic sighing\" — improved mood and lowered respiratory rate more than mindfulness meditation over one month.","simple":"Five minutes of long, slow out-breaths a day can lift mood.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"carpenter2018","authors":["Carpenter JK","Andrews LA","Witcraft SM","Powers MB","Smits JAJ","Hofmann SG"],"year":2018,"title":"Cognitive behavioral therapy for anxiety and related disorders: A meta‐analysis of randomized placebo‐controlled trials","journal":"Depression and Anxiety","volume":"35","issue":"6","pages":"502–514","doi":"10.1002/da.22728","pmid":"29451967","type":"Meta-analysis","n":"41 RCTs, 2,843 patients","effect":"g = 0.56 vs placebo","areas":["anxiety"],"finding":"CBT had moderate placebo-controlled effects on target anxiety symptoms (g = 0.56), smaller effects on depression and quality of life, and almost tripled the odds of response (OR 2.97).","simple":"Compared with placebo, CBT substantially improves anxiety and related problems.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"zaccaro2018","authors":["Zaccaro A","Piarulli A","Laurino M","Garbella E","Menicucci D","Neri B","Gemignani A"],"year":2018,"title":"How Breath-Control Can Change Your Life: A Systematic Review on Psycho-Physiological Correlates of Slow Breathing","journal":"Frontiers in Human Neuroscience","volume":"12","issue":"","pages":"353","doi":"10.3389/fnhum.2018.00353","pmid":"30245619","type":"Systematic review","n":"15 studies","effect":"","areas":["anxiety"],"finding":"Slow breathing (under 10 breaths per minute) increased heart-rate variability and parasympathetic activity and was associated with comfort, relaxation, vigour and reduced anxiety and arousal.","simple":"Slowing your breathing measurably calms your body.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"craske2014","authors":["Craske MG","Treanor M","Conway CC","Zbozinek T","Vervliet B"],"year":2014,"title":"Maximizing exposure therapy: An inhibitory learning approach","journal":"Behaviour Research and Therapy","volume":"58","issue":"","pages":"10–23","doi":"10.1016/j.brat.2014.04.006","pmid":"24864005","type":"Theoretical review","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["anxiety"],"finding":"Maximising exposure via inhibitory learning: (1) expectancy violation, (2) deepened extinction, (3) occasional reinforced extinction, (4) removal of safety signals, (5) variability, (6) retrieval cues, (7) multiple contexts, (8) affect labelling.","simple":"Exposure works best when you test your fears, drop \"safety crutches\" and practise in varied situations.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"mayowilson2014","authors":["Mayo-Wilson E","Dias S","Mavranezouli I","Kew K","Clark DM","Ades AE","Pilling S"],"year":2014,"title":"Psychological and pharmacological interventions for social anxiety disorder in adults: a systematic review and network meta-analysis","journal":"The Lancet Psychiatry","volume":"1","issue":"5","pages":"368–376","doi":"10.1016/s2215-0366(14)70329-3","pmid":"26361000","type":"Network meta-analysis","n":"101 trials, 13,164 participants","effect":"","areas":["anxiety"],"finding":"Individual CBT had the largest effects of the psychological treatments for social anxiety disorder and was recommended as the best initial treatment.","simple":"One-to-one CBT is the first-choice treatment for social anxiety.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"cuijpers2014gad","authors":["Cuijpers P","Sijbrandij M","Koole S","Huibers M","Berking M","Andersson G"],"year":2014,"title":"Psychological treatment of generalized anxiety disorder: A meta-analysis","journal":"Clinical Psychology Review","volume":"34","issue":"2","pages":"130–140","doi":"10.1016/j.cpr.2014.01.002","pmid":"24487344","type":"Meta-analysis","n":"41 studies, 2,132 patients","effect":"g = 0.84 vs control","areas":["anxiety"],"finding":"Psychotherapy for generalised anxiety disorder (mostly CBT) had a large effect compared with control conditions (g = 0.84) and also reduced depression (g = 0.71).","simple":"CBT is very effective for chronic worry.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"mcgowan2013","authors":["McGowan SK","Behar E"],"year":2013,"title":"A Preliminary Investigation of Stimulus Control Training for Worry: Effects on Anxiety and Insomnia","journal":"Behavior Modification","volume":"37","issue":"1","pages":"90–112","doi":"10.1177/0145445512455661","pmid":"22977265","type":"Randomized controlled trial","n":"53 high worriers","effect":"","areas":["anxiety"],"finding":"Two weeks of a daily 30-minute time- and place-restricted worry period reduced worry, anxiety, negative affect and insomnia compared with a control condition.","simple":"Two weeks of scheduled worry time reduced worry and improved sleep.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"kircanski2012","authors":["Kircanski K","Lieberman MD","Craske MG"],"year":2012,"title":"Feelings Into Words: Contributions of Language to Exposure Therapy","journal":"Psychological Science","volume":"23","issue":"10","pages":"1086–1091","doi":"10.1177/0956797612443830","pmid":"22902568","type":"Randomized experiment","n":"Spider-fearful adults","effect":"","areas":["anxiety"],"finding":"During exposure, labelling one’s fear led to smaller physiological fear responses a week later than reappraisal, distraction or exposure alone; using more anxiety words predicted larger reductions.","simple":"Saying what you feel while facing a fear helps the learning stick.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"nice-cg113","authors":["National Institute for Health and Care Excellence"],"year":2011,"title":"Generalised anxiety disorder and panic disorder in adults: management (NICE clinical guideline CG113)","publisher":"NICE","type":"Clinical guideline","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/cg113","areas":["anxiety","general"],"finding":"Stepped care: psychoeducation and guided or non-facilitated self-help based on CBT first, then high-intensity CBT or applied relaxation for generalised anxiety; CBT for panic disorder.","simple":"UK guidance recommends CBT-based self-help first for anxiety, then therapy if needed.","journal":"","volume":"","issue":"","pages":"","doi":"","pmid":"","n":"—","effect":"","verified":"Official source"},
  {"id":"mcmillan2010","authors":["McMillan D","Lee R"],"year":2010,"title":"A systematic review of behavioral experiments vs. exposure alone in the treatment of anxiety disorders: A case of exposure while wearing the emperor's new clothes?","journal":"Clinical Psychology Review","volume":"30","issue":"5","pages":"467–478","doi":"10.1016/j.cpr.2010.01.003","pmid":"20381224","type":"Systematic review","n":"14 studies, N = 644","effect":"","areas":["anxiety"],"finding":"Across OCD, panic, social anxiety and specific phobias, there was some evidence that behavioural experiments (testing a specific belief) were more effective than exposure alone.","simple":"Treating a fear like a hypothesis to test may work even better than plain exposure.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"hofmann2008","authors":["Hofmann SG","Smits JAJ"],"year":2008,"title":"Cognitive-behavioral therapy for adult anxiety disorders: A meta-analysis of randomized placebo-controlled trials","journal":"The Journal of Clinical Psychiatry","volume":"69","issue":"4","pages":"621–632","doi":"10.4088/jcp.v69n0415","pmid":"18363421","type":"Meta-analysis","n":"27 placebo-controlled RCTs","effect":"g = 0.73 vs placebo","areas":["anxiety"],"finding":"CBT outperformed placebo for adult anxiety disorders (g = 0.73 on anxiety severity; response odds ratio 4.06). The strongest effects were in OCD and acute stress disorder, the weakest in panic disorder.","simple":"CBT works for anxiety disorders even when compared with a convincing placebo.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"craske2008","authors":["Craske MG","Kircanski K","Zelikowsky M","Mystkowski J","Chowdhury N","Baker A"],"year":2008,"title":"Optimizing inhibitory learning during exposure therapy","journal":"Behaviour Research and Therapy","volume":"46","issue":"1","pages":"5–27","doi":"10.1016/j.brat.2007.10.003","pmid":"18005936","type":"Review","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["anxiety"],"finding":"How much fear drops within an exposure session does not reliably predict long-term outcome; lasting change comes from new learning that the feared outcome does not happen and that fear can be tolerated.","simple":"You do not need to feel calm by the end of a practice — what counts is learning your fear was wrong.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"wolitzkytaylor2008","authors":["Wolitzky-Taylor KB","Horowitz JD","Powers MB","Telch MJ"],"year":2008,"title":"Psychological approaches in the treatment of specific phobias: A meta-analysis","journal":"Clinical Psychology Review","volume":"28","issue":"6","pages":"1021–1037","doi":"10.1016/j.cpr.2008.02.007","pmid":"18410984","type":"Meta-analysis","n":"33 RCTs","effect":"","areas":["anxiety"],"finding":"Exposure-based treatment produced large effects for specific phobias, outperforming placebo and other psychotherapies; real-life (in vivo) exposure beat imaginal or virtual exposure at post-treatment.","simple":"Facing feared situations gradually is the most effective treatment for phobias.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"manzoni2008","authors":["Manzoni GM","Pagnini F","Castelnuovo G","Molinari E"],"year":2008,"title":"Relaxation training for anxiety: a ten-years systematic review with meta-analysis","journal":"BMC Psychiatry","volume":"8","issue":"1","pages":"41","doi":"10.1186/1471-244x-8-41","pmid":"18518981","type":"Meta-analysis","n":"27 studies","effect":"d = 0.51–0.57","areas":["anxiety"],"finding":"Relaxation training reduced anxiety with a medium effect (d ≈ 0.51–0.57); efficacy was higher with meditation and longer training.","simple":"Regular relaxation practice reliably lowers anxiety.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"lieberman2007","authors":["Lieberman MD","Eisenberger NI","Crockett MJ","Tom SM","Pfeifer JH","Way BM"],"year":2007,"title":"Putting Feelings Into Words","journal":"Psychological Science","volume":"18","issue":"5","pages":"421–428","doi":"10.1111/j.1467-9280.2007.01916.x","pmid":"17576282","type":"fMRI experiment","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["anxiety","impulsiveness"],"finding":"Putting feelings into words (affect labelling) reduced amygdala responses to emotional images.","simple":"Naming an emotion (\"I feel anxious\") calms the brain’s alarm system.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"dugas2007","authors":["Dugas MJ","Robichaud M"],"year":2007,"title":"Cognitive-Behavioral Treatment for Generalized Anxiety Disorder: From Science to Practice","publisher":"Routledge","type":"Treatment manual","areas":["anxiety"],"finding":"Treatment manual for the intolerance-of-uncertainty model of GAD, including worry awareness, uncertainty experiments, problem-solving and written exposure.","simple":"A structured programme for chronic worry.","journal":"","volume":"","issue":"","pages":"","doi":"","pmid":"","n":"—","effect":"","verified":"Bibliographic record"},
  {"id":"spitzer2006","authors":["Spitzer RL","Kroenke K","Williams JBW","Löwe B"],"year":2006,"title":"A Brief Measure for Assessing Generalized Anxiety Disorder: The GAD-7","journal":"Archives of Internal Medicine","volume":"166","issue":"10","pages":"1092","doi":"10.1001/archinte.166.10.1092","pmid":"16717171","type":"Validation study","n":"2,740 primary-care patients; 965 interviewed","effect":"Sensitivity 89%, specificity 82%","areas":["anxiety","general"],"finding":"The 7-item GAD-7 had good reliability and validity. The optimal cut-point (10) gave 89% sensitivity and 82% specificity for generalised anxiety disorder; higher scores tracked with more functional impairment.","simple":"The GAD-7 is a short, well-tested questionnaire for tracking worry and anxiety.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"clark2006","authors":["Clark DM","Ehlers A","Hackmann A","McManus F","Fennell M","Grey N","Waddington L","Wild J"],"year":2006,"title":"Cognitive therapy versus exposure and applied relaxation in social phobia: A randomized controlled trial","journal":"Journal of Consulting and Clinical Psychology","volume":"74","issue":"3","pages":"568–578","doi":"10.1037/0022-006x.74.3.568","pmid":"16822113","type":"Randomized controlled trial","n":"62 patients","effect":"84% vs 42% recovered","areas":["anxiety"],"finding":"Cognitive therapy for social phobia beat exposure plus applied relaxation: 84% vs 42% no longer met diagnostic criteria (0% on wait-list).","simple":"A CBT approach that targets self-focus and safety behaviours helped most people with social anxiety recover.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"craske2006","authors":["Craske MG","Barlow DH"],"year":2006,"title":"Mastery of Your Anxiety and Panic: Workbook (4th ed.)","publisher":"Oxford University Press","type":"Self-help workbook","areas":["anxiety"],"finding":"The workbook for Panic Control Treatment, including psychoeducation, breathing, cognitive restructuring, interoceptive exposure and situational exposure.","simple":"The evidence-based self-help workbook for panic.","journal":"","volume":"","issue":"","pages":"","doi":"","pmid":"","n":"—","effect":"","verified":"Bibliographic record"},
  {"id":"bennettlevy2004","authors":["Bennett-Levy J","Butler G","Fennell M","Hackmann A","Mueller M","Westbrook D"],"year":2004,"title":"Oxford Guide to Behavioural Experiments in Cognitive Therapy","publisher":"Oxford University Press","type":"Clinical guide","areas":["anxiety","depression"],"finding":"The standard clinical guide to designing behavioural experiments: state the belief, make a prediction, plan the test, record the result and draw a new conclusion.","simple":"How to test your fears like a scientist.","journal":"","volume":"","issue":"","pages":"","doi":"","pmid":"","n":"—","effect":"","verified":"Bibliographic record"},
  {"id":"ladouceur2000","authors":["Ladouceur R","Dugas MJ","Freeston MH","Léger E","Gagnon F","Thibodeau N"],"year":2000,"title":"Efficacy of a cognitive–behavioral treatment for generalized anxiety disorder: Evaluation in a controlled clinical trial","journal":"Journal of Consulting and Clinical Psychology","volume":"68","issue":"6","pages":"957–964","doi":"10.1037/0022-006x.68.6.957","pmid":"","type":"Randomized controlled trial","n":"26 GAD patients","effect":"77% no longer met criteria","areas":["anxiety"],"finding":"CBT targeting intolerance of uncertainty produced clinically significant change maintained at 6 and 12 months; 77% of participants no longer met criteria for generalised anxiety disorder.","simple":"Learning to tolerate uncertainty helped most people with chronic worry recover.","verified":"Crossref + OpenAlex abstract"},
  {"id":"barlow2000","authors":["Barlow DH","Gorman JM","Shear MK","Woods SW"],"year":2000,"title":"Cognitive-Behavioral Therapy, Imipramine, or Their Combination for Panic Disorder","journal":"JAMA","volume":"283","issue":"19","pages":"2529","doi":"10.1001/jama.283.19.2529","pmid":"10815116","type":"Randomized controlled trial","n":"312 patients with panic disorder","effect":"","areas":["anxiety"],"finding":"CBT and imipramine were each superior to placebo for panic disorder; after 6 months of maintenance both remained more effective than placebo (response ~40% vs 13%).","simple":"CBT works as well as medication for panic disorder.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"dugas1998","authors":["Dugas MJ","Gagnon F","Ladouceur R","Freeston MH"],"year":1998,"title":"Generalized anxiety disorder: a preliminary test of a conceptual model","journal":"Behaviour Research and Therapy","volume":"36","issue":"2","pages":"215–226","doi":"10.1016/s0005-7967(97)00070-3","pmid":"9613027","type":"Model test","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["anxiety"],"finding":"Intolerance of uncertainty, positive beliefs about worry, poor problem orientation and cognitive avoidance distinguished people with GAD — the foundation of the intolerance-of-uncertainty model of worry.","simple":"Chronic worry is driven by finding uncertainty unbearable.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"wells1995","authors":["Wells A","Clark DM","Salkovskis P","Ludgate J","Hackmann A","Gelder M"],"year":1995,"title":"Social phobia: The role of in-situation safety behaviors in maintaining anxiety and negative beliefs","journal":"Behavior Therapy","volume":"26","issue":"1","pages":"153–161","doi":"10.1016/s0005-7894(05)80088-7","pmid":"","type":"Experiment","n":"People with social phobia","effect":"","areas":["anxiety"],"finding":"Exposure combined with dropping in-situation safety behaviours reduced anxiety and belief in feared outcomes more than exposure alone.","simple":"Facing a fear works better when you also drop your safety habits.","verified":"Crossref"},
  {"id":"salkovskis1991","authors":["Salkovskis PM"],"year":1991,"title":"The Importance of Behaviour in the Maintenance of Anxiety and Panic: A Cognitive Account","journal":"Behavioural Psychotherapy","volume":"19","issue":"1","pages":"6–19","doi":"10.1017/s0141347300011472","pmid":"","type":"Theoretical paper","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["anxiety"],"finding":"Safety-seeking behaviours (e.g., holding on to something, avoiding eye contact) prevent people from discovering that the feared catastrophe would not have happened, so anxiety persists.","simple":"The little things you do to feel safe can stop you learning that you were safe anyway.","verified":"Crossref + OpenAlex abstract"},
  {"id":"ost1989","authors":["Öst LG"],"year":1989,"title":"One-session treatment for specific phobias","journal":"Behaviour Research and Therapy","volume":"27","issue":"1","pages":"1–7","doi":"10.1016/0005-7967(89)90113-7","pmid":"2914000","type":"Case series","n":"20 patients","effect":"90% much improved/recovered","areas":["anxiety"],"finding":"A single session (average 2.1 hours) of in-vivo exposure plus modelling left 90% of patients with specific phobias much improved or completely recovered at about 4-year follow-up.","simple":"For some phobias, one long session of facing the fear can be enough.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"ost1987","authors":["Öst LG"],"year":1987,"title":"Applied relaxation: Description of a coping technique and review of controlled studies","journal":"Behaviour Research and Therapy","volume":"25","issue":"5","pages":"397–409","doi":"10.1016/0005-7967(87)90017-9","pmid":"3318800","type":"Technique & review","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["anxiety"],"finding":"Applied relaxation teaches a quick relaxation response (eventually within 20–30 seconds) that is then applied in anxiety-provoking situations; controlled studies supported it for panic, phobias and GAD.","simple":"Relaxation becomes a portable skill you can use the moment anxiety rises.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"borkovec1983","authors":["Borkovec TD","Wilkinson L","Folensbee R","Lerman C"],"year":1983,"title":"Stimulus control applications to the treatment of worry","journal":"Behaviour Research and Therapy","volume":"21","issue":"3","pages":"247–251","doi":"10.1016/0005-7967(83)90206-1","pmid":"6615390","type":"Experiment","n":"Chronic worriers","effect":"","areas":["anxiety"],"finding":"Stimulus-control instructions — postponing worry to a fixed daily 30-minute worry period in the same place — reduced the amount of time spent worrying.","simple":"Saving worries for a set \"worry time\" reduces how much you worry.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"biwer2023","authors":["Biwer F","Wiradhany W","oude Egbrink MGA","de Bruin ABH"],"year":2023,"title":"Understanding effort regulation: Comparing ‘Pomodoro’ breaks and self‐regulated breaks","journal":"British Journal of Educational Psychology","volume":"93","issue":"S2","pages":"353–367","doi":"10.1111/bjep.12593","pmid":"36859717","type":"Experiment","n":"University students","effect":"","areas":["productivity"],"finding":"Students taking self-regulated breaks reported more fatigue and distractedness and less concentration and motivation than those taking pre-set, systematic (\"Pomodoro\") breaks; systematic breaks achieved similar task completion in less time.","simple":"Timed work sprints with planned breaks kept students fresher and more focused.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"ruizpardo2022","authors":["Ruiz Pardo AC","Minda JP"],"year":2022,"title":"Reexamining the “brain drain” effect: A replication of Ward et al. (2017)","journal":"Acta Psychologica","volume":"230","issue":"","pages":"103717","doi":"10.1016/j.actpsy.2022.103717","pmid":"36007374","type":"Replication study","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["productivity"],"finding":"A replication using the same tasks as Ward et al. (2017) found no effect of smartphone location on working memory or inhibition — the \"brain drain\" effect did not replicate.","simple":"A careful re-test did not find that a nearby phone drains brainpower.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"wang2021","authors":["Wang G","Wang Y","Gai X"],"year":2021,"title":"A Meta-Analysis of the Effects of Mental Contrasting With Implementation Intentions on Goal Attainment","journal":"Frontiers in Psychology","volume":"12","issue":"","pages":"565202","doi":"10.3389/fpsyg.2021.565202","pmid":"34054628","type":"Meta-analysis","n":"21 studies, 24 effect sizes, 15,907 participants","effect":"g = 0.34","areas":["productivity","self-discipline"],"finding":"Mental contrasting with implementation intentions (MCII/WOOP) improved goal attainment with a small-to-medium effect (g = 0.336); effects were larger when delivered interactively with a person (g = 0.47) than via documents (g = 0.28).","simple":"Imagining your goal AND the obstacle in your way — then planning for it — helps you reach goals.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"rozental2018","authors":["Rozental A","Forsström D","Lindner P","Nilsson S","Mårtensson L","Rizzo A","Andersson G","Carlbring P"],"year":2018,"title":"Treating Procrastination Using Cognitive Behavior Therapy: A Pragmatic Randomized Controlled Trial Comparing Treatment Delivered via the Internet or in Groups","journal":"Behavior Therapy","volume":"49","issue":"2","pages":"180–197","doi":"10.1016/j.beth.2017.08.002","pmid":"29530258","type":"Randomized controlled trial","n":"Adults with severe procrastination","effect":"Within-group d ≈ 1.2–1.3","areas":["productivity"],"finding":"Both self-guided internet CBT and group CBT produced large reductions in procrastination (d = 1.29 and 1.24); group participants maintained or extended gains at follow-up while some self-guided participants slipped.","simple":"CBT strongly reduced severe procrastination; doing it with others helped people keep their gains.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"rozental2018meta","authors":["Rozental A","Bennett S","Forsström D","Ebert DD","Shafran R","Andersson G","Carlbring P"],"year":2018,"title":"Targeting Procrastination Using Psychological Treatments: A Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis","journal":"Frontiers in Psychology","volume":"9","issue":"","pages":"1588","doi":"10.3389/fpsyg.2018.01588","pmid":"30214421","type":"Systematic review & meta-analysis","n":"Controlled trials of procrastination treatments","effect":"g = 0.34 overall; CBT g = 0.55","areas":["productivity"],"finding":"Psychological treatments reduced procrastination compared with inactive controls (g = 0.34). CBT showed a moderate benefit (g = 0.55) but was based on only a few studies.","simple":"Procrastination can be treated, and CBT looks like one of the most promising approaches.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"epton2017","authors":["Epton T","Currie S","Armitage CJ"],"year":2017,"title":"Unique effects of setting goals on behavior change: Systematic review and meta-analysis","journal":"Journal of Consulting and Clinical Psychology","volume":"85","issue":"12","pages":"1182–1198","doi":"10.1037/ccp0000260","pmid":"29189034","type":"Meta-analysis","n":"141 papers, 384 effect sizes, N = 16,523","effect":"d = 0.34","areas":["productivity","self-discipline"],"finding":"Goal setting had a small-to-medium unique effect on behaviour change (d = .34), larger when goals were difficult, set publicly and set as a group goal.","simple":"Setting a goal works — even better if it is ambitious and shared.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"ward2017","authors":["Ward AF","Duke K","Gneezy A","Bos MW"],"year":2017,"title":"Brain Drain: The Mere Presence of One’s Own Smartphone Reduces Available Cognitive Capacity","journal":"Journal of the Association for Consumer Research","volume":"2","issue":"2","pages":"140–154","doi":"10.1086/691462","pmid":"34504505","type":"Experiments","n":"2 experiments","effect":"","areas":["productivity"],"finding":"The mere presence of one’s own smartphone appeared to reduce available working memory and fluid intelligence, even when the phone was off. (A later direct replication did not find this effect.)","simple":"Your phone nearby might drain focus — but the evidence is mixed.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"harkin2016","authors":["Harkin B","Webb TL","Chang BPI","Prestwich A","Conner M","Kellar I","Benn Y","Sheeran P"],"year":2016,"title":"Does monitoring goal progress promote goal attainment? A meta-analysis of the experimental evidence","journal":"Psychological Bulletin","volume":"142","issue":"2","pages":"198–229","doi":"10.1037/bul0000025","pmid":"26479070","type":"Meta-analysis","n":"138 studies, N = 19,951","effect":"d = 0.40","areas":["productivity","self-discipline","general"],"finding":"Interventions that increased monitoring of goal progress improved goal attainment (d = 0.40), especially when progress was physically recorded or reported publicly.","simple":"Writing down your progress makes you more likely to reach your goal.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"sheeran2016","authors":["Sheeran P","Webb TL"],"year":2016,"title":"The Intention–Behavior Gap","journal":"Social and Personality Psychology Compass","volume":"10","issue":"9","pages":"503–518","doi":"10.1111/spc3.12265","pmid":"","type":"Review","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["productivity","self-discipline"],"finding":"Intentions often fail to translate into action; failures cluster around not getting started, not staying on track and not disengaging from unhelpful goals. Planning, monitoring and habit strategies help close the gap.","simple":"Good intentions are not enough — you need plans and systems.","verified":"Crossref + OpenAlex abstract"},
  {"id":"rozental2015","authors":["Rozental A","Forsell E","Svensson A","Andersson G","Carlbring P"],"year":2015,"title":"Internet-based cognitive—behavior therapy for procrastination: A randomized controlled trial","journal":"Journal of Consulting and Clinical Psychology","volume":"83","issue":"4","pages":"808–824","doi":"10.1037/ccp0000023","pmid":"25939016","type":"Randomized controlled trial","n":"150 participants","effect":"d up to 0.81 vs waitlist","areas":["productivity"],"finding":"Guided and unguided internet CBT both reduced procrastination compared with a waitlist (d = 0.50–0.81); 31–40% of guided participants achieved clinically significant change.","simple":"An online CBT programme helped people procrastinate less, with or without a therapist.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"sirois2013","authors":["Sirois F","Pychyl T"],"year":2013,"title":"Procrastination and the Priority of Short‐Term Mood Regulation: Consequences for Future Self","journal":"Social and Personality Psychology Compass","volume":"7","issue":"2","pages":"115–127","doi":"10.1111/spc3.12011","pmid":"","type":"Theoretical review","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["productivity"],"finding":"Procrastination is a form of short-term mood repair: people avoid tasks to escape the negative emotions those tasks trigger, prioritising present mood over the needs of their future self.","simple":"Procrastination is less about laziness and more about avoiding bad feelings.","verified":"Crossref + OpenAlex abstract"},
  {"id":"oettingen2012","authors":["Oettingen G"],"year":2012,"title":"Future thought and behaviour change","journal":"European Review of Social Psychology","volume":"23","issue":"1","pages":"1–63","doi":"10.1080/10463283.2011.643698","pmid":"","type":"Review","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["productivity","self-discipline"],"finding":"Positive fantasies about the future alone can sap energy; mentally contrasting the desired future with present obstacles creates commitment when success is likely and helps people let go when it is not.","simple":"Dreaming alone does not get things done — dream, then face the obstacle.","verified":"Crossref + OpenAlex abstract"},
  {"id":"duckworth2011","authors":["Duckworth AL","Grant H","Loew B","Oettingen G","Gollwitzer PM"],"year":2011,"title":"Self‐regulation strategies improve self‐discipline in adolescents: benefits of mental contrasting and implementation intentions","journal":"Educational Psychology","volume":"31","issue":"1","pages":"17–26","doi":"10.1080/01443410.2010.506003","pmid":"","type":"Randomized controlled trial","n":"66 high-school students","effect":"60%+ more practice questions","areas":["productivity","self-discipline"],"finding":"Students who completed a 30-minute written MCII exercise completed more than 60% more practice exam questions than students in a placebo writing condition.","simple":"A half-hour planning exercise led students to do 60% more study.","verified":"Crossref + OpenAlex abstract"},
  {"id":"amabile2011","authors":["Amabile TM","Kramer SJ"],"year":2011,"title":"The Progress Principle: Using Small Wins to Ignite Joy, Engagement, and Creativity at Work","publisher":"Harvard Business Review Press","type":"Book (diary study)","areas":["productivity"],"finding":"Analysis of daily diaries from knowledge workers: making progress in meaningful work was the most important driver of positive inner work life on a given day.","simple":"Noticing small wins is one of the biggest motivators at work.","journal":"","volume":"","issue":"","pages":"","doi":"","pmid":"","n":"—","effect":"","verified":"Bibliographic record"},
  {"id":"wohl2010","authors":["Wohl MJ","Pychyl TA","Bennett SH"],"year":2010,"title":"I forgive myself, now I can study: How self-forgiveness for procrastinating can reduce future procrastination","journal":"Personality and Individual Differences","volume":"48","issue":"7","pages":"803–808","doi":"10.1016/j.paid.2010.01.029","pmid":"","type":"Longitudinal study","n":"First-year university students","effect":"","areas":["productivity","self-discipline"],"finding":"Students who forgave themselves for procrastinating on studying for a first exam procrastinated less when preparing for the next exam.","simple":"Being kind to yourself about procrastinating reduces procrastination next time.","verified":"Crossref"},
  {"id":"mark2008","authors":["Mark G","Gudith D","Klocke U"],"year":2008,"title":"The cost of interrupted work","journal":"Proceedings of the SIGCHI Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems","volume":"","issue":"","pages":"107–110","doi":"10.1145/1357054.1357072","pmid":"","type":"Experiment","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["productivity","time-management"],"finding":"People compensated for interruptions by working faster with no loss of quality, but at the cost of more stress, frustration, time pressure and effort.","simple":"Interruptions make you rush and stress, even if the work still gets done.","verified":"Crossref + OpenAlex abstract"},
  {"id":"steel2007","authors":["Steel P"],"year":2007,"title":"The nature of procrastination: A meta-analytic and theoretical review of quintessential self-regulatory failure","journal":"Psychological Bulletin","volume":"133","issue":"1","pages":"65–94","doi":"10.1037/0033-2909.133.1.65","pmid":"17201571","type":"Meta-analysis","n":"691 correlations","effect":"","areas":["productivity"],"finding":"The strongest, most consistent predictors of procrastination were task aversiveness, delay until rewards, low self-efficacy and impulsiveness, plus low conscientiousness (self-control, distractibility, organisation, achievement motivation) — consistent with temporal motivation theory.","simple":"We put off tasks that feel unpleasant, pay off later, feel hard to succeed at — especially when we are easily distracted.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"steel2006","authors":["Steel P","König CJ"],"year":2006,"title":"Integrating Theories of Motivation","journal":"Academy of Management Review","volume":"31","issue":"4","pages":"889–913","doi":"10.5465/amr.2006.22527462","pmid":"","type":"Theory","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["productivity"],"finding":"Temporal Motivation Theory integrates expectancy, value, impulsiveness and delay: Motivation = (Expectancy × Value) / (1 + Impulsiveness × Delay).","simple":"Motivation rises when you believe you can succeed and care about the result, and falls when rewards are far away and distractions are close.","verified":"Crossref + OpenAlex abstract"},
  {"id":"gollwitzer2006","authors":["Gollwitzer PM","Sheeran P"],"year":2006,"title":"Implementation Intentions and Goal Achievement: A Meta‐analysis of Effects and Processes","journal":"Advances in Experimental Social Psychology","volume":"","issue":"","pages":"69–119","doi":"10.1016/s0065-2601(06)38002-1","pmid":"","type":"Meta-analysis","n":"94 independent tests","effect":"d = 0.65","areas":["productivity","self-discipline","impulsiveness"],"finding":"Forming implementation intentions (\"If situation X, then I will do Y\") had a medium-to-large effect on goal attainment (d = 0.65), helping with getting started, staying on track and resisting temptation.","simple":"Deciding exactly when, where and how you will act makes you far more likely to follow through.","verified":"Crossref"},
  {"id":"webb2006","authors":["Webb TL","Sheeran P"],"year":2006,"title":"Does changing behavioral intentions engender behavior change? A meta-analysis of the experimental evidence","journal":"Psychological Bulletin","volume":"132","issue":"2","pages":"249–268","doi":"10.1037/0033-2909.132.2.249","pmid":"16536643","type":"Meta-analysis","n":"47 experimental tests","effect":"Intention d = 0.66 → behaviour d = 0.36","areas":["productivity","self-discipline"],"finding":"A medium-to-large change in intention (d = 0.66) produced only a small-to-medium change in behaviour (d = 0.36) — motivation alone does not reliably change behaviour.","simple":"Wanting something more only gets you part of the way.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"ferrari2005","authors":["Ferrari JR","O’Callaghan J","Newbegin I"],"year":2005,"title":"Prevalence of procrastination in the United States, United Kingdom, and Australia: Arousal and avoidance delays among adults","journal":"North American Journal of Psychology","type":"Cross-national survey","areas":["productivity"],"finding":"Around one in five adults in the US, UK and Australia identified as chronic procrastinators.","simple":"About 1 in 5 adults are chronic procrastinators.","volume":"","issue":"","pages":"","doi":"","pmid":"","n":"—","effect":"","verified":"Bibliographic record"},
  {"id":"locke2002","authors":["Locke EA","Latham GP"],"year":2002,"title":"Building a practically useful theory of goal setting and task motivation: A 35-year odyssey","journal":"American Psychologist","volume":"57","issue":"9","pages":"705–717","doi":"10.1037/0003-066x.57.9.705","pmid":"12237980","type":"Review","n":"35 years of research","effect":"","areas":["productivity","time-management"],"finding":"Specific, challenging goals produce higher performance than vague \"do your best\" goals, provided there is commitment, feedback and an adequate strategy.","simple":"Clear, specific goals beat \"I’ll try my best\".","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"gollwitzer1999","authors":["Gollwitzer PM"],"year":1999,"title":"Implementation intentions: Strong effects of simple plans","journal":"American Psychologist","volume":"54","issue":"7","pages":"493–503","doi":"10.1037/0003-066x.54.7.493","pmid":"","type":"Review","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["productivity","self-discipline"],"finding":"Implementation intentions delegate control to situational cues, so the planned action starts almost automatically when the cue appears.","simple":"An if–then plan turns a good intention into an automatic response.","verified":"Crossref + OpenAlex abstract"},
  {"id":"tice1997","authors":["Tice DM","Baumeister RF"],"year":1997,"title":"Longitudinal Study of Procrastination, Performance, Stress, and Health: The Costs and Benefits of Dawdling","journal":"Psychological Science","volume":"8","issue":"6","pages":"454–458","doi":"10.1111/j.1467-9280.1997.tb00460.x","pmid":"","type":"Longitudinal studies","n":"2 student samples","effect":"","areas":["productivity","time-management"],"finding":"Procrastinators reported less stress and illness early in the semester but more stress and illness late in the term, were sicker overall, and received lower grades on all assignments.","simple":"Putting things off feels better now and much worse later.","verified":"Crossref + OpenAlex abstract"},
  {"id":"kirgios2020","authors":["Kirgios EL","Mandel GH","Park Y","Milkman KL","Gromet DM","Kay JS","Duckworth AL"],"year":2020,"title":"Teaching temptation bundling to boost exercise: A field experiment","journal":"Organizational Behavior and Human Decision Processes","volume":"161","issue":"","pages":"20–35","doi":"10.1016/j.obhdp.2020.09.003","pmid":"","type":"Field experiment","n":"6,792 participants","effect":"+10–14% weekly workout likelihood","areas":["self-discipline"],"finding":"Giving people audiobooks and encouraging temptation bundling raised their likelihood of working out each week by 10–14% during and up to 17 weeks after the programme.","simple":"Pairing exercise with a favourite audiobook boosted workouts in a large real-world study.","verified":"Crossref + OpenAlex abstract"},
  {"id":"duckworth2016","authors":["Duckworth AL","Gendler TS","Gross JJ"],"year":2016,"title":"Situational Strategies for Self-Control","journal":"Perspectives on Psychological Science","volume":"11","issue":"1","pages":"35–55","doi":"10.1177/1745691615623247","pmid":"26817725","type":"Review","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["self-discipline","productivity","impulsiveness"],"finding":"Situational self-control strategies — selecting or modifying situations before temptation arises — are often more effective and less effortful than resisting impulses in the moment.","simple":"Change your surroundings so you do not have to rely on willpower.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"wood2016","authors":["Wood W","Rünger D"],"year":2016,"title":"Psychology of Habit","journal":"Annual Review of Psychology","volume":"67","issue":"1","pages":"289–314","doi":"10.1146/annurev-psych-122414-033417","pmid":"26361052","type":"Review","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["self-discipline"],"finding":"Habits are context–response associations learned through repetition with reward; they are best changed by altering cues and friction rather than relying on willpower or information.","simple":"Make good habits easy and bad habits hard.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"hagger2016","authors":["Hagger MS","Chatzisarantis NLD","Alberts H","Anggono CO","Batailler C","Birt AR","Brand R","Brandt MJ","Brewer G","Bruyneel S","Calvillo DP","Campbell WK","Cannon PR","Carlucci M","Carruth NP","Cheung T","Crowell A","De Ridder DTD","Dewitte S","Elson M","Evans JR","Fay BA","Fennis BM","Finley A","Francis Z","Heise E","Hoemann H","Inzlicht M","Koole SL","Koppel L","Kroese F","Lange F","Lau K","Lynch BP","Martijn C","Merckelbach H","Mills NV","Michirev A","Miyake A","Mosser AE","Muise M","Muller D","Muzi M","Nalis D","Nurwanti R","Otgaar H","Philipp MC","Primoceri P","Rentzsch K","Ringos L","Schlinkert C","Schmeichel BJ","Schoch SF","Schrama M","Schütz A","Stamos A","Tinghög G","Ullrich J","vanDellen M","Wimbarti S","Wolff W","Yusainy C","Zerhouni O","Zwienenberg M"],"year":2016,"title":"A Multilab Preregistered Replication of the Ego-Depletion Effect","journal":"Perspectives on Psychological Science","volume":"11","issue":"4","pages":"546–573","doi":"10.1177/1745691616652873","pmid":"27474142","type":"Multi-lab preregistered replication","n":"23 labs, N = 2,141","effect":"d = 0.04","areas":["self-discipline"],"finding":"The ego-depletion effect was close to zero (d = 0.04, 95% CI −0.07 to 0.15), challenging the idea that self-control is a limited resource that runs out.","simple":"Willpower probably does not \"run out\" like a fuel tank.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"galla2015","authors":["Galla BM","Duckworth AL"],"year":2015,"title":"More than resisting temptation: Beneficial habits mediate the relationship between self-control and positive life outcomes","journal":"Journal of Personality and Social Psychology","volume":"109","issue":"3","pages":"508–525","doi":"10.1037/pspp0000026","pmid":"25643222","type":"Six studies","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["self-discipline"],"finding":"Beneficial habits mediated the link between self-control and positive outcomes such as homework completion, GPA and college persistence — more than effortful inhibition did.","simple":"Self-control pays off mainly through building good habits.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"atjak2015","authors":["A-Tjak JG","Davis ML","Morina N","Powers MB","Smits JA","Emmelkamp PM"],"year":2015,"title":"A Meta-Analysis of the Efficacy of Acceptance and Commitment Therapy for Clinically Relevant Mental and Physical Health Problems","journal":"Psychotherapy and Psychosomatics","volume":"84","issue":"1","pages":"30–36","doi":"10.1159/000365764","pmid":"25547522","type":"Meta-analysis","n":"39 RCTs, 1,821 patients","effect":"g = 0.57 vs control","areas":["self-discipline","general"],"finding":"Acceptance and Commitment Therapy (values + acceptance + committed action) outperformed control conditions (g = 0.57) and was comparable to established treatments such as CBT.","simple":"Acting on your values, even with discomfort, is an effective therapeutic approach.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"milkman2014","authors":["Milkman KL","Minson JA","Volpp KGM"],"year":2014,"title":"Holding the Hunger Games Hostage at the Gym: An Evaluation of Temptation Bundling","journal":"Management Science","volume":"60","issue":"2","pages":"283–299","doi":"10.1287/mnsc.2013.1784","pmid":"25843979","type":"Field experiment","n":"Gym members","effect":"+51% gym visits initially","areas":["self-discipline"],"finding":"Restricting tempting audiobooks to the gym (\"temptation bundling\") increased gym visits by 51% initially (29% for a lighter version), though effects declined over time; 61% later paid for gym-only access to their audiobooks.","simple":"Only letting yourself enjoy a treat while doing a chore makes you do the chore more.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"dai2014","authors":["Dai H","Milkman KL","Riis J"],"year":2014,"title":"The Fresh Start Effect: Temporal Landmarks Motivate Aspirational Behavior","journal":"Management Science","volume":"60","issue":"10","pages":"2563–2582","doi":"10.1287/mnsc.2014.1901","pmid":"","type":"Field studies","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["self-discipline"],"finding":"Temporal landmarks — a new week, month, year or birthday — increased aspirational behaviour such as gym visits and goal commitments (\"fresh start effect\").","simple":"Mondays, new months and birthdays are great moments to start a change.","verified":"Crossref"},
  {"id":"vangelder2013","authors":["van Gelder JL","Hershfield HE","Nordgren LF"],"year":2013,"title":"Vividness of the Future Self Predicts Delinquency","journal":"Psychological Science","volume":"24","issue":"6","pages":"974–980","doi":"10.1177/0956797612465197","pmid":"23592649","type":"Experiments","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["self-discipline","impulsiveness"],"finding":"A more vivid sense of the future self reduced self-reported delinquency and cheating; vividness of the future self supports self-control.","simple":"Picturing your future self clearly helps you resist short-term temptations.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"gardner2012","authors":["Gardner B","Lally P","Wardle J"],"year":2012,"title":"Making health habitual: the psychology of ‘habit-formation’ and general practice","journal":"British Journal of General Practice","volume":"62","issue":"605","pages":"664–666","doi":"10.3399/bjgp12x659466","pmid":"23211256","type":"Review","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["self-discipline"],"finding":"Habit formation advice for practice: pick a simple action, do it in the same context every day, and repetition will gradually make it automatic — habits then persist even when motivation fades.","simple":"Do a small action at the same time and place every day and it becomes automatic.","verified":"Crossref + OpenAlex abstract"},
  {"id":"deridder2012","authors":["de Ridder DTD","Lensvelt-Mulders G","Finkenauer C","Stok FM","Baumeister RF"],"year":2012,"title":"Taking Stock of Self-Control","journal":"Personality and Social Psychology Review","volume":"16","issue":"1","pages":"76–99","doi":"10.1177/1088868311418749","pmid":"21878607","type":"Meta-analysis","n":"102 studies, N = 32,648","effect":"","areas":["self-discipline","impulsiveness"],"finding":"Trait self-control related to a wide range of behaviours, and more strongly to automatic (habitual) behaviours than to controlled ones — people with high self-control rely on good routines.","simple":"Disciplined people mostly have better habits, not more willpower.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"hofmann2012temptation","authors":["Hofmann W","Baumeister RF","Förster G","Vohs KD"],"year":2012,"title":"Everyday temptations: An experience sampling study of desire, conflict, and self-control","journal":"Journal of Personality and Social Psychology","volume":"102","issue":"6","pages":"1318–1335","doi":"10.1037/a0026545","pmid":"22149456","type":"Experience sampling study","n":"205 adults, one week of beeper reports","effect":"","areas":["self-discipline","impulsiveness"],"finding":"Desires were frequent in everyday life and often conflicted with other goals. Personality mattered most for how strong and conflicting desires were; situational factors mattered more for whether people resisted or acted.","simple":"Temptations are everywhere — and your situation strongly shapes whether you give in.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"breines2012","authors":["Breines JG","Chen S"],"year":2012,"title":"Self-Compassion Increases Self-Improvement Motivation","journal":"Personality and Social Psychology Bulletin","volume":"38","issue":"9","pages":"1133–1143","doi":"10.1177/0146167212445599","pmid":"22645164","type":"Experiments","n":"4 experiments","effect":"","areas":["self-discipline","productivity"],"finding":"Self-compassion after a failure or weakness increased motivation to improve: more study time after failing, more motivation to make amends and to change weaknesses.","simple":"Being kind to yourself after a slip makes you try harder, not less.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"hershfield2011","authors":["Hershfield HE","Goldstein DG","Sharpe WF","Fox J","Yeykelis L","Carstensen LL","Bailenson JN"],"year":2011,"title":"Increasing Saving Behavior Through Age-Progressed Renderings of the Future Self","journal":"Journal of Marketing Research","volume":"48","issue":"SPL","pages":"S23–S37","doi":"10.1509/jmkr.48.spl.s23","pmid":"24634544","type":"Experiments","n":"4 studies","effect":"","areas":["self-discipline","impulsiveness"],"finding":"People who interacted with realistic, age-progressed images of their future selves were more willing to choose later, larger rewards over immediate ones.","simple":"Feeling connected to your future self makes it easier to invest in them.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"moffitt2011","authors":["Moffitt TE","Arseneault L","Belsky D","Dickson N","Hancox RJ","Harrington H","Houts R","Poulton R","Roberts BW","Ross S","Sears MR","Thomson WM","Caspi A"],"year":2011,"title":"A gradient of childhood self-control predicts health, wealth, and public safety","journal":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","volume":"108","issue":"7","pages":"2693–2698","doi":"10.1073/pnas.1010076108","pmid":"21262822","type":"Birth cohort study","n":"1,000 children followed to age 32","effect":"","areas":["self-discipline","impulsiveness"],"finding":"Childhood self-control predicted adult physical health, substance dependence, finances and criminal offending along a gradient, independent of IQ and social class.","simple":"Self-control in childhood predicts health, wealth and wellbeing decades later — and it can be learned.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"lally2010","authors":["Lally P","van Jaarsveld CHM","Potts HWW","Wardle J"],"year":2010,"title":"How are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world","journal":"European Journal of Social Psychology","volume":"40","issue":"6","pages":"998–1009","doi":"10.1002/ejsp.674","pmid":"","type":"Longitudinal study","n":"96 volunteers over 12 weeks","effect":"Median 66 days (range 18–254)","areas":["self-discipline"],"finding":"Automaticity of a new daily behaviour grew along a curve and plateaued after a median of 66 days (range 18–254 days). Missing one opportunity did not materially affect habit formation.","simple":"New habits take about two months on average to feel automatic — and missing a day is fine.","verified":"Crossref + OpenAlex abstract"},
  {"id":"bryan2010","authors":["Bryan G","Karlan D","Nelson S"],"year":2010,"title":"Commitment Devices","journal":"Annual Review of Economics","volume":"2","issue":"1","pages":"671–698","doi":"10.1146/annurev.economics.102308.124324","pmid":"","type":"Review","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["self-discipline","impulsiveness"],"finding":"Commitment devices — arrangements that make it costly or impossible to abandon a goal later — help people who know they are prone to present bias.","simple":"Lock in your future self’s choice while you are thinking clearly.","verified":"Crossref + OpenAlex abstract"},
  {"id":"gine2010","authors":["Giné X","Karlan D","Zinman J"],"year":2010,"title":"Put Your Money Where Your Butt Is: A Commitment Contract for Smoking Cessation","journal":"American Economic Journal: Applied Economics","volume":"2","issue":"4","pages":"213–235","doi":"10.1257/app.2.4.213","pmid":"","type":"Randomized controlled trial","n":"Smokers in the Philippines","effect":"+3 percentage points quit rate","areas":["self-discipline","impulsiveness"],"finding":"Smokers offered a voluntary commitment contract (forfeit savings if they failed a nicotine test) were 3 percentage points more likely to pass at 6 months, and the effect persisted in surprise tests at 12 months; 11% took up the offer.","simple":"Putting money on the line helped smokers quit for good.","verified":"Crossref + OpenAlex abstract"},
  {"id":"job2010","authors":["Job V","Dweck CS","Walton GM"],"year":2010,"title":"Ego depletion — Is it all in your head? Implicit theories about willpower affect self-regulation","journal":"Psychological Science","volume":"21","issue":"11","pages":"1686–1693","doi":"10.1177/0956797610384745","pmid":"20876879","type":"Experiments & field study","n":"4 studies","effect":"","areas":["self-discipline"],"finding":"Only people who believed willpower is limited showed reduced self-control after demanding tasks; beliefs about willpower predicted eating, procrastination and goal striving under stress.","simple":"Believing your willpower is limited makes it more limited.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"wilson2010","authors":["Wilson KG","Sandoz EK","Kitchens J","Roberts M"],"year":2010,"title":"The Valued Living Questionnaire: Defining and Measuring Valued Action within a Behavioral Framework","journal":"The Psychological Record","volume":"60","issue":"2","pages":"249–272","doi":"10.1007/bf03395706","pmid":"","type":"Measure development","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["self-discipline"],"finding":"The Valued Living Questionnaire asks how important different life domains are and how consistently one has acted on them; the gap guides values-based action.","simple":"Comparing what matters to you with how you actually spend your time shows where to act.","verified":"Crossref"},
  {"id":"wood2007","authors":["Wood W","Neal DT"],"year":2007,"title":"A new look at habits and the habit-goal interface","journal":"Psychological Review","volume":"114","issue":"4","pages":"843–863","doi":"10.1037/0033-295x.114.4.843","pmid":"17907866","type":"Theory","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["self-discipline"],"finding":"Habits are triggered by context cues and run largely independently of current goals; changing cues and environments is key to changing habits.","simple":"Habits are set off by your surroundings, so change the surroundings.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"duckworth2005","authors":["Duckworth AL","Seligman ME"],"year":2005,"title":"Self-Discipline Outdoes IQ in Predicting Academic Performance of Adolescents","journal":"Psychological Science","volume":"16","issue":"12","pages":"939–944","doi":"10.1111/j.1467-9280.2005.01641.x","pmid":"16313657","type":"Longitudinal study","n":"Two cohorts of eighth-graders","effect":"","areas":["self-discipline"],"finding":"Self-discipline accounted for more than twice as much variance as IQ in final grades, school attendance, homework hours and time of starting homework.","simple":"Self-discipline predicted school success better than IQ did.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"tangney2004","authors":["Tangney JP","Baumeister RF","Boone AL"],"year":2004,"title":"High Self‐Control Predicts Good Adjustment, Less Pathology, Better Grades, and Interpersonal Success","journal":"Journal of Personality","volume":"72","issue":"2","pages":"271–324","doi":"10.1111/j.0022-3506.2004.00263.x","pmid":"15016066","type":"Measure development & correlational","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["self-discipline"],"finding":"Higher scores on the Self-Control Scale predicted better grades, adjustment, interpersonal relationships and fewer psychological problems.","simple":"Self-control is linked to success in almost every area of life.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"gross1998","authors":["Gross JJ"],"year":1998,"title":"The Emerging Field of Emotion Regulation: An Integrative Review","journal":"Review of General Psychology","volume":"2","issue":"3","pages":"271–299","doi":"10.1037/1089-2680.2.3.271","pmid":"","type":"Review","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["self-discipline","impulsiveness","anxiety"],"finding":"The process model of emotion regulation: situation selection, situation modification, attentional deployment, cognitive change and response modulation.","simple":"You can manage feelings early (choosing situations) or late (managing reactions) — earlier is usually easier.","verified":"Crossref + OpenAlex abstract"},
  {"id":"marlatt1985","authors":["Marlatt GA","Gordon JR"],"year":1985,"title":"Relapse Prevention: Maintenance Strategies in the Treatment of Addictive Behaviors","publisher":"Guilford Press","type":"Book","areas":["self-discipline","impulsiveness"],"finding":"Introduced relapse prevention and the \"abstinence violation effect\": after a lapse, all-or-nothing thinking (\"I’ve blown it\") turns a slip into a full relapse.","simple":"A slip is not a failure — how you respond to it decides what happens next.","journal":"","volume":"","issue":"","pages":"","doi":"","pmid":"","n":"—","effect":"","verified":"Bibliographic record"},
  {"id":"aeon2021","authors":["Aeon B","Faber A","Panaccio A"],"year":2021,"title":"Does time management work? A meta-analysis","journal":"PLOS ONE","volume":"16","issue":"1","pages":"e0245066","doi":"10.1371/journal.pone.0245066","pmid":"33428644","type":"Meta-analysis","n":"158 samples","effect":"","areas":["time-management"],"finding":"Time management was moderately related to job performance, academic achievement and wellbeing, and negatively to distress. It enhanced wellbeing — especially life satisfaction — more than it enhanced performance.","simple":"Managing your time well makes you happier and less stressed — even more than it makes you productive.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"zhu2018","authors":["Zhu M","Yang Y","Hsee CK"],"year":2018,"title":"The Mere Urgency Effect","journal":"Journal of Consumer Research","volume":"45","issue":"3","pages":"673–690","doi":"10.1093/jcr/ucy008","pmid":"","type":"Experiments","n":"5 studies","effect":"","areas":["time-management"],"finding":"People choose urgent tasks over important ones even when the important task has an objectively better payoff — the \"mere urgency effect\".","simple":"Urgent things hijack our attention even when important things matter more.","verified":"Crossref + OpenAlex abstract"},
  {"id":"scullin2018","authors":["Scullin MK","Krueger ML","Ballard HK","Pruett N","Bliwise DL"],"year":2018,"title":"The effects of bedtime writing on difficulty falling asleep: A polysomnographic study comparing to-do lists and completed activity lists","journal":"Journal of Experimental Psychology: General","volume":"147","issue":"1","pages":"139–146","doi":"10.1037/xge0000374","pmid":"29058942","type":"Randomized polysomnography study","n":"57 young adults","effect":"","areas":["time-management","depression"],"finding":"Writing a to-do list for 5 minutes at bedtime led to falling asleep significantly faster than writing about completed activities; the more specific the list, the faster people fell asleep.","simple":"A 5-minute to-do list before bed can help you fall asleep faster.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"hafner2015","authors":["Häfner A","Stock A","Oberst V"],"year":2015,"title":"Decreasing students’ stress through time management training: an intervention study","journal":"European Journal of Psychology of Education","volume":"30","issue":"1","pages":"81–94","doi":"10.1007/s10212-014-0229-2","pmid":"","type":"Intervention study","n":"University students","effect":"","areas":["time-management"],"finding":"Time-management training decreased students’ perceived stress and increased their perceived control of time compared with a control group.","simple":"Students who learned time-management skills felt less stressed.","verified":"Crossref"},
  {"id":"masicampo2011","authors":["Masicampo EJ","Baumeister RF"],"year":2011,"title":"Consider it done! Plan making can eliminate the cognitive effects of unfulfilled goals","journal":"Journal of Personality and Social Psychology","volume":"101","issue":"4","pages":"667–683","doi":"10.1037/a0024192","pmid":"21688924","type":"Experiments","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["time-management","productivity"],"finding":"Unfinished goals produce intrusive thoughts, but making a specific plan for them eliminated those intrusions and freed cognitive resources.","simple":"Writing down a plan for an unfinished task lets your mind stop nagging you about it.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"hafner2010","authors":["Häfner A","Stock A"],"year":2010,"title":"Time Management Training and Perceived Control of Time at Work","journal":"The Journal of Psychology","volume":"144","issue":"5","pages":"429–447","doi":"10.1080/00223980.2010.496647","pmid":"20806849","type":"Randomized controlled trial","n":"71 employees","effect":"","areas":["time-management"],"finding":"Time-management training increased perceived control of time and decreased perceived stress, without measurable effects on performance indicators.","simple":"A short time-management course lowered stress.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"leroy2009","authors":["Leroy S"],"year":2009,"title":"Why is it so hard to do my work? The challenge of attention residue when switching between work tasks","journal":"Organizational Behavior and Human Decision Processes","volume":"109","issue":"2","pages":"168–181","doi":"10.1016/j.obhdp.2009.04.002","pmid":"","type":"Experiments","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["time-management","productivity"],"finding":"Leaving a task unfinished to start another leaves \"attention residue\" that lowers performance on the next task.","simple":"Part of your mind stays on the last task when you switch.","verified":"Crossref"},
  {"id":"heylighen2008","authors":["Heylighen F","Vidal C"],"year":2008,"title":"Getting Things Done: The Science behind Stress-Free Productivity","journal":"Long Range Planning","volume":"41","issue":"6","pages":"585–605","doi":"10.1016/j.lrp.2008.09.004","pmid":"","type":"Theoretical analysis","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["time-management","productivity"],"finding":"A cognitive-science analysis of the \"Getting Things Done\" method: capturing tasks in an external trusted system and defining next actions reduces memory load and worry.","simple":"Getting tasks out of your head and into a system frees up mental space.","verified":"Crossref"},
  {"id":"claessens2007","authors":["Claessens BJ","van Eerde W","Rutte CG","Roe RA"],"year":2007,"title":"A review of the time management literature","journal":"Personnel Review","volume":"36","issue":"2","pages":"255–276","doi":"10.1108/00483480710726136","pmid":"","type":"Literature review","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["time-management"],"finding":"Time-management behaviours were associated with perceived control of time, job satisfaction, health and lower stress; links with job performance were less clear.","simple":"Feeling in control of your time is the main benefit of time management.","verified":"Crossref + OpenAlex abstract"},
  {"id":"schmidt2007","authors":["Schmidt C","Collette F","Cajochen C","Peigneux P"],"year":2007,"title":"A time to think: Circadian rhythms in human cognition","journal":"Cognitive Neuropsychology","volume":"24","issue":"7","pages":"755–789","doi":"10.1080/02643290701754158","pmid":"18066734","type":"Review","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["time-management","productivity"],"finding":"Cognitive performance varies across the day with circadian rhythms and chronotype; performance is best when tasks are aligned with one’s peak time (\"synchrony effect\").","simple":"Schedule your hardest thinking work at the time of day you feel sharpest.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"kruger2004","authors":["Kruger J","Evans M"],"year":2004,"title":"If you don't want to be late, enumerate: Unpacking reduces the planning fallacy","journal":"Journal of Experimental Social Psychology","volume":"40","issue":"5","pages":"586–598","doi":"10.1016/j.jesp.2003.11.001","pmid":"","type":"Experiments","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["time-management","productivity"],"finding":"Breaking a task into its component steps (\"unpacking\") before estimating produced longer, more accurate time predictions and reduced the planning fallacy.","simple":"List all the steps before you guess how long something will take.","verified":"Crossref"},
  {"id":"monsell2003","authors":["Monsell S"],"year":2003,"title":"Task switching","journal":"Trends in Cognitive Sciences","volume":"7","issue":"3","pages":"134–140","doi":"10.1016/s1364-6613(03)00028-7","pmid":"12639695","type":"Review","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["time-management","productivity"],"finding":"Switching between tasks carries a reliable time cost, which preparation reduces but does not eliminate.","simple":"Every switch between tasks costs time.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"rubinstein2001","authors":["Rubinstein JS","Meyer DE","Evans JE"],"year":2001,"title":"Executive control of cognitive processes in task switching","journal":"Journal of Experimental Psychology: Human Perception and Performance","volume":"27","issue":"4","pages":"763–797","doi":"10.1037/0096-1523.27.4.763","pmid":"11518143","type":"Experiments","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["time-management","productivity"],"finding":"Task-switching costs grow with task complexity and unfamiliarity, reflecting time needed to reconfigure goals and rules.","simple":"Switching between complicated tasks is especially costly.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"macan1994","authors":["Macan TH"],"year":1994,"title":"Time management: Test of a process model","journal":"Journal of Applied Psychology","volume":"79","issue":"3","pages":"381–391","doi":"10.1037/0021-9010.79.3.381","pmid":"","type":"Field study","n":"Employees in a variety of jobs","effect":"","areas":["time-management"],"finding":"Time-management behaviours worked through perceived control of time to reduce job tension and increase satisfaction; effects on supervisor-rated performance were not found.","simple":"Planning helps mainly by making you feel in control.","verified":"Crossref + OpenAlex abstract"},
  {"id":"buehler1994","authors":["Buehler R","Griffin D","Ross M"],"year":1994,"title":"Exploring the \"planning fallacy\": Why people underestimate their task completion times","journal":"Journal of Personality and Social Psychology","volume":"67","issue":"3","pages":"366–381","doi":"10.1037/0022-3514.67.3.366","pmid":"","type":"Experiments","n":"5 studies","effect":"Predicted 33.9 days vs actual 55.5 days","areas":["time-management","productivity"],"finding":"People underestimate their own (but not others’) completion times. Students predicted their senior thesis would take 33.9 days on average; it took 55.5. Connecting predictions to past experiences removed the bias.","simple":"We nearly always think tasks will take less time than they do — check how long similar tasks took before.","verified":"Crossref + OpenAlex abstract"},
  {"id":"mallory2019","authors":["Mallory AB","Stanton AM","Handy AB"],"year":2019,"title":"Couples’ Sexual Communication and Dimensions of Sexual Function: A Meta-Analysis","journal":"The Journal of Sex Research","volume":"56","issue":"7","pages":"882–898","doi":"10.1080/00224499.2019.1568375","pmid":"30777780","type":"Meta-analysis","n":"48 studies","effect":"r = .35 with overall sexual function","areas":["sexual-desire"],"finding":"Couples’ sexual communication was positively associated with desire (r = .16), arousal, orgasm and overall sexual function (r = .35).","simple":"Couples who talk openly about sex have better sex lives.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"hallberg2019","authors":["Hallberg J","Kaldo V","Arver S","Dhejne C","Jokinen J","Öberg KG"],"year":2019,"title":"A Randomized Controlled Study of Group-Administered Cognitive Behavioral Therapy for Hypersexual Disorder in Men","journal":"The Journal of Sexual Medicine","volume":"16","issue":"5","pages":"733–745","doi":"10.1016/j.jsxm.2019.03.005","pmid":"30956109","type":"Randomized controlled trial","n":"137 men","effect":"","areas":["sexual-desire","impulsiveness"],"finding":"Seven weeks of group CBT reduced hypersexual disorder symptoms and sexual compulsivity and improved psychiatric well-being compared with waitlist, with gains stable at 3 and 6 months.","simple":"CBT helped men regain control over compulsive sexual behaviour.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"grubbs2019","authors":["Grubbs JB","Perry SL","Wilt JA","Reid RC"],"year":2019,"title":"Pornography Problems Due to Moral Incongruence: An Integrative Model with a Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis","journal":"Archives of Sexual Behavior","volume":"48","issue":"2","pages":"397–415","doi":"10.1007/s10508-018-1248-x","pmid":"30076491","type":"Systematic review & meta-analysis","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["sexual-desire","impulsiveness"],"finding":"Feeling \"addicted\" to pornography is often better explained by moral incongruence — a conflict between one’s values and behaviour — than by the amount of use.","simple":"Distress about porn often comes from a clash with your values, not just how much you use.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"kraus2018","authors":["Kraus SW","Krueger RB","Briken P","First MB","Stein DJ","Kaplan MS","Voon V","Abdo CH","Grant JE","Atalla E","Reed GM"],"year":2018,"title":"Compulsive sexual behaviour disorder in the ICD‐11","journal":"World Psychiatry","volume":"17","issue":"1","pages":"109–110","doi":"10.1002/wps.20499","pmid":"29352554","type":"Diagnostic classification","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["sexual-desire","impulsiveness"],"finding":"ICD-11 includes Compulsive Sexual Behaviour Disorder as an impulse-control disorder: a persistent pattern of failing to control intense, repetitive sexual urges, causing marked distress or impairment.","simple":"Out-of-control sexual behaviour is a recognised, treatable condition.","verified":"Crossref + OpenAlex abstract"},
  {"id":"brotto2018book","authors":["Brotto LA"],"year":2018,"title":"Better Sex Through Mindfulness: How Women Can Cultivate Desire","publisher":"Greystone Books","type":"Self-help book","areas":["sexual-desire"],"finding":"Translates the mindfulness-based sex therapy programme tested in Brotto’s clinical trials into a self-help format.","simple":"The mindfulness programme from the trials, written for self-help.","journal":"","volume":"","issue":"","pages":"","doi":"","pmid":"","n":"—","effect":"","verified":"Bibliographic record"},
  {"id":"stephenson2017","authors":["Stephenson KR","Kerth J"],"year":2017,"title":"Effects of Mindfulness-Based Therapies for Female Sexual Dysfunction: A Meta-Analytic Review","journal":"The Journal of Sex Research","volume":"54","issue":"7","pages":"832–849","doi":"10.1080/00224499.2017.1331199","pmid":"28617103","type":"Meta-analysis","n":"11 trials, 449 participants","effect":"","areas":["sexual-desire"],"finding":"Mindfulness-based therapies improved all aspects of women’s sexual function and sexual well-being with generally moderate effects; controlled comparisons were fewer and less certain.","simple":"Mindfulness reliably helps women’s sexual wellbeing, though more controlled trials are needed.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"brotto2017asexuality","authors":["Brotto LA","Yule M"],"year":2017,"title":"Asexuality: Sexual Orientation, Paraphilia, Sexual Dysfunction, or None of the Above?","journal":"Archives of Sexual Behavior","volume":"46","issue":"3","pages":"619–627","doi":"10.1007/s10508-016-0802-7","pmid":"27542079","type":"Review","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["sexual-desire"],"finding":"There is insufficient evidence to classify asexuality as a psychiatric condition or disorder of desire; it likely meets criteria for a sexual orientation.","simple":"Little or no sexual attraction is not a disorder if it does not bother you — it may be your orientation.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"graham2017","authors":["Graham CA","Mercer CH","Tanton C","Jones KG","Johnson AM","Wellings K","Mitchell KR"],"year":2017,"title":"What factors are associated with reporting lacking interest in sex and how do these vary by gender? Findings from the third British national survey of sexual attitudes and lifestyles","journal":"BMJ Open","volume":"7","issue":"9","pages":"e016942","doi":"10.1136/bmjopen-2017-016942","pmid":"28903968","type":"National probability survey (Natsal-3)","n":"Sexually active adults in Britain","effect":"15.0% of men, 34.2% of women","areas":["sexual-desire"],"finding":"15.0% of men and 34.2% of women reported lacking interest in sex for at least three months in the past year; it was linked to relationship length, difficulty communicating, mismatched interest and health.","simple":"Low interest in sex is very common — around 1 in 3 women and 1 in 7 men experience it.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"muise2016","authors":["Muise A","Schimmack U","Impett EA"],"year":2016,"title":"Sexual Frequency Predicts Greater Well-Being, But More is Not Always Better","journal":"Social Psychological and Personality Science","volume":"7","issue":"4","pages":"295–302","doi":"10.1177/1948550615616462","pmid":"","type":"3 studies","n":"N = 30,645","effect":"","areas":["sexual-desire"],"finding":"In relationships, sexual frequency was associated with well-being in a curvilinear way: no further benefit beyond about once a week.","simple":"More is not always better — quality and connection matter more than frequency.","verified":"Crossref + OpenAlex abstract"},
  {"id":"crosby2016","authors":["Crosby JM","Twohig MP"],"year":2016,"title":"Acceptance and Commitment Therapy for Problematic Internet Pornography Use: A Randomized Trial","journal":"Behavior Therapy","volume":"47","issue":"3","pages":"355–366","doi":"10.1016/j.beth.2016.02.001","pmid":"27157029","type":"Randomized controlled trial","n":"28 men","effect":"93% vs 21% reduction","areas":["sexual-desire","impulsiveness"],"finding":"Eight sessions of Acceptance and Commitment Therapy reduced problematic pornography viewing by 93% versus 21% on the waitlist; at 3 months 74% maintained at least a 70% reduction.","simple":"Learning to accept urges without acting on them sharply reduced problematic porn use.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"nagoski2015","authors":["Nagoski E"],"year":2015,"title":"Come As You Are: The Surprising New Science That Will Transform Your Sex Life","publisher":"Simon & Schuster","type":"Popular science book","areas":["sexual-desire"],"finding":"Accessible explanation of the Dual Control Model (\"brakes and accelerators\"), responsive desire and the role of context and stress.","simple":"A readable guide to brakes, accelerators and responsive desire.","journal":"","volume":"","issue":"","pages":"","doi":"","pmid":"","n":"—","effect":"","verified":"Bibliographic record"},
  {"id":"weiner2014","authors":["Weiner L","Avery-Clark C"],"year":2014,"title":"Sensate Focus: clarifying the Masters and Johnson's model","journal":"Sexual and Relationship Therapy","volume":"29","issue":"3","pages":"307–319","doi":"10.1080/14681994.2014.892920","pmid":"","type":"Clinical review","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["sexual-desire"],"finding":"Sensate focus means mindful, non-demand touching for one’s own interest — not \"pleasuring\" a partner — so that pressure to perform is removed and natural responses can return.","simple":"Touch with curiosity and no goals; take the pressure off and let responses come naturally.","verified":"Crossref + OpenAlex abstract"},
  {"id":"brotto2014","authors":["Brotto LA","Basson R"],"year":2014,"title":"Group mindfulness-based therapy significantly improves sexual desire in women","journal":"Behaviour Research and Therapy","volume":"57","issue":"","pages":"43–54","doi":"10.1016/j.brat.2014.04.001","pmid":"24814472","type":"Controlled trial","n":"117 women","effect":"","areas":["sexual-desire"],"finding":"Mindfulness-based group therapy significantly improved sexual desire, arousal, lubrication, satisfaction and overall function; increases in mindfulness and reductions in depression predicted better desire.","simple":"Training attention to stay in the moment improved desire for women with low desire.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"lorenz2014","authors":["Lorenz TA","Meston CM"],"year":2014,"title":"EXERCISE IMPROVES SEXUAL FUNCTION IN WOMEN TAKING ANTIDEPRESSANTS: RESULTS FROM A RANDOMIZED CROSSOVER TRIAL","journal":"Depression and Anxiety","volume":"31","issue":"3","pages":"188–195","doi":"10.1002/da.22208","pmid":"24754044","type":"Randomized crossover trial","n":"Women on antidepressants","effect":"","areas":["sexual-desire"],"finding":"Exercise immediately before sexual activity improved sexual desire (and global function in women with dysfunction); scheduling regular sexual activity improved orgasm function.","simple":"A short workout before intimacy improved desire for women on antidepressants.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"fruhauf2013","authors":["Frühauf S","Gerger H","Schmidt HM","Munder T","Barth J"],"year":2013,"title":"Efficacy of Psychological Interventions for Sexual Dysfunction: A Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis","journal":"Archives of Sexual Behavior","volume":"42","issue":"6","pages":"915–933","doi":"10.1007/s10508-012-0062-0","pmid":"23559141","type":"Meta-analysis","n":"RCTs of psychological treatments","effect":"d = 0.58 symptoms; d = 0.47 satisfaction","areas":["sexual-desire"],"finding":"Psychological interventions reduced sexual dysfunction symptom severity (d = 0.58) and increased sexual satisfaction (d = 0.47); good evidence exists for female hypoactive sexual desire disorder and female orgasmic disorder.","simple":"Talking therapies effectively improve sexual problems, including low desire.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"muise2013","authors":["Muise A","Impett EA","Kogan A","Desmarais S"],"year":2013,"title":"Keeping the spark alive: Being motivated to meet a partner’s sexual needs sustains sexual desire in long-term romantic relationships","journal":"Social Psychological and Personality Science","volume":"4","issue":"3","pages":"267–273","doi":"10.1177/1948550612457185","pmid":"","type":"Daily diary & longitudinal study","n":"Couples in long-term relationships","effect":"","areas":["sexual-desire"],"finding":"People motivated to meet their partner’s sexual needs (\"sexual communal strength\") reported higher daily desire and were buffered against declines in desire over four months.","simple":"Caring about each other’s needs helps keep desire alive.","verified":"Crossref + OpenAlex abstract"},
  {"id":"mitchell2013","authors":["Mitchell KR","Mercer CH","Ploubidis GB","Jones KG","Datta J","Field N","Copas AJ","Tanton C","Erens B","Sonnenberg P","Clifton S","Macdowall W","Phelps A","Johnson AM","Wellings K"],"year":2013,"title":"Sexual function in Britain: findings from the third National Survey of Sexual Attitudes and Lifestyles (Natsal-3)","journal":"The Lancet","volume":"382","issue":"9907","pages":"1817–1829","doi":"10.1016/s0140-6736(13)62366-1","pmid":"24286787","type":"National probability survey (Natsal-3)","n":"4,913 men and 6,777 women","effect":"","areas":["sexual-desire"],"finding":"Low sexual function was associated with depression, poor health, relationship unhappiness, the end of a relationship and inability to talk easily about sex with a partner.","simple":"Mood, health and relationship communication all strongly influence sexual wellbeing.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"bancroft2009","authors":["Bancroft J","Graham CA","Janssen E","Sanders SA"],"year":2009,"title":"The Dual Control Model: Current Status and Future Directions","journal":"The Journal of Sex Research","volume":"46","issue":"2-3","pages":"121–142","doi":"10.1080/00224490902747222","pmid":"19308839","type":"Review","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["sexual-desire"],"finding":"Reviews research on the Dual Control Model, including measures of excitation/inhibition, effects of mood, ageing and gender, and its relevance to sexual problems and their treatment.","simple":"Stress, mood and worries often act as \"brakes\" on desire.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"serretti2009","authors":["Serretti A","Chiesa A"],"year":2009,"title":"Treatment-emergent sexual dysfunction related to antidepressants: A meta-analysis","journal":"Journal of Clinical Psychopharmacology","volume":"29","issue":"3","pages":"259–266","doi":"10.1097/jcp.0b013e3181a5233f","pmid":"19440080","type":"Meta-analysis","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["sexual-desire"],"finding":"Many antidepressants (e.g., sertraline, venlafaxine, citalopram, paroxetine, fluoxetine) caused treatment-emergent sexual dysfunction in 25.8–80.3% of patients; agomelatine, bupropion, mirtazapine, moclobemide and others did not differ from placebo.","simple":"Medication side-effects are a common, treatable cause of low desire — talk to your prescriber.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"brotto2008","authors":["Brotto LA","Basson R","Luria M"],"year":2008,"title":"A Mindfulness-Based Group Psychoeducational Intervention Targeting Sexual Arousal Disorder in Women","journal":"The Journal of Sexual Medicine","volume":"5","issue":"7","pages":"1646–1659","doi":"10.1111/j.1743-6109.2008.00850.x","pmid":"18507718","type":"Pilot trial","n":"Women with desire/arousal complaints","effect":"","areas":["sexual-desire"],"finding":"A brief three-session mindfulness-based psychoeducational group improved desire, arousal and satisfaction; participants rated mindfulness as the most helpful component.","simple":"A short mindfulness course improved women’s desire and arousal.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"shifren2008","authors":["Shifren JL","Monz BU","Russo PA","Segreti A","Johannes CB"],"year":2008,"title":"Sexual Problems and Distress in United States Women: Prevalence and Correlates","journal":"Obstetrics & Gynecology","volume":"112","issue":"5","pages":"970–978","doi":"10.1097/aog.0b013e3181898cdb","pmid":"18978095","type":"Population survey","n":"31,581 US women","effect":"43.1% any problem; 12.0% distressing","areas":["sexual-desire"],"finding":"Sexual problems were reported by 43.1% of women, but distressing sexual problems by 12.0%, peaking in midlife — distress, not just low desire, defines a problem worth treating.","simple":"Changes in desire are very common; they only count as a problem if they bother you.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"nobre2006","authors":["Nobre PJ","Pinto‐Gouveia J"],"year":2006,"title":"Dysfunctional sexual beliefs as vulnerability factors for sexual dysfunction","journal":"Journal of Sex Research","volume":"43","issue":"1","pages":"68–75","doi":"10.1080/00224490609552300","pmid":"16817069","type":"Cross-sectional study","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["sexual-desire"],"finding":"Dysfunctional sexual beliefs (for example about age, body image and performance) were vulnerability factors for sexual dysfunction in men and women.","simple":"Unhelpful beliefs about sex and bodies can reduce desire — and beliefs can be changed.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"basson2000","authors":["Basson R"],"year":2000,"title":"The Female Sexual Response: A Different Model","journal":"Journal of Sex & Marital Therapy","volume":"26","issue":"1","pages":"51–65","doi":"10.1080/009262300278641","pmid":"10693116","type":"Theoretical model","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["sexual-desire"],"finding":"A circular model in which desire is often responsive — arising after arousal begins in a context of intimacy and willingness — rather than spontaneous, to avoid labelling normal variation as dysfunction.","simple":"For many people, desire shows up after things get started, not before. That is normal.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"bancroft2000","authors":["Bancroft J","Janssen E"],"year":2000,"title":"The dual control model of male sexual response: a theoretical approach to centrally mediated erectile dysfunction","journal":"Neuroscience & Biobehavioral Reviews","volume":"24","issue":"5","pages":"571–579","doi":"10.1016/s0149-7634(00)00024-5","pmid":"10880822","type":"Theoretical model","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["sexual-desire"],"finding":"The Dual Control Model: sexual response depends on the balance between a sexual excitation system (\"accelerator\") and a sexual inhibition system (\"brakes\").","simple":"Desire depends on pressing the accelerator AND easing off the brakes.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"aron2000","authors":["Aron A","Norman CC","Aron EN","McKenna C","Heyman RE"],"year":2000,"title":"Couples' shared participation in novel and arousing activities and experienced relationship quality","journal":"Journal of Personality and Social Psychology","volume":"78","issue":"2","pages":"273–284","doi":"10.1037/0022-3514.78.2.273","pmid":"10707334","type":"Experiments","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["sexual-desire"],"finding":"Couples who shared novel and arousing activities reported greater increases in relationship quality than couples doing mundane activities.","simple":"Doing new, exciting things together re-energises a relationship.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"barlow1986","authors":["Barlow DH"],"year":1986,"title":"Causes of sexual dysfunction: The role of anxiety and cognitive interference","journal":"Journal of Consulting and Clinical Psychology","volume":"54","issue":"2","pages":"140–148","doi":"10.1037/0022-006x.54.2.140","pmid":"3700800","type":"Review","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["sexual-desire"],"finding":"In sexual dysfunction, anxiety combined with distraction by performance concerns (\"spectatoring\") interferes with arousal, while people without dysfunction focus on erotic cues.","simple":"Watching and judging yourself during sex blocks arousal — attention is key.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"masters1970","authors":["Masters WH","Johnson VE"],"year":1970,"title":"Human Sexual Inadequacy","publisher":"Little, Brown","type":"Book","areas":["sexual-desire"],"finding":"Introduced sensate focus and the concept of \"spectatoring\" (self-monitoring during sex) as a cause of sexual difficulties.","simple":"The origin of sensate focus — touch without pressure.","journal":"","volume":"","issue":"","pages":"","doi":"","pmid":"","n":"—","effect":"","verified":"Bibliographic record"},
  {"id":"storebo2020","authors":["Storebø OJ","Stoffers-Winterling JM","Völlm BA","Kongerslev MT","Mattivi JT","Jørgensen MS","Faltinsen E","Todorovac A","Sales CP","Callesen HE","Lieb K","Simonsen E"],"year":2020,"title":"Psychological therapies for people with borderline personality disorder","journal":"Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews","volume":"2020","issue":"5","pages":"CD012955","doi":"10.1002/14651858.cd012955.pub2","pmid":"32368793","type":"Cochrane systematic review","n":"75 RCTs, 4,507 participants","effect":"","areas":["impulsiveness"],"finding":"Psychotherapy reduced borderline personality disorder severity versus usual care (SMD −0.52); DBT showed benefits for severity, self-harm and functioning, though evidence quality was low.","simple":"Structured skills therapies like DBT help people manage intense emotions and impulses.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"rung2018","authors":["Rung JM","Madden GJ"],"year":2018,"title":"Experimental reductions of delay discounting and impulsive choice: A systematic review and meta-analysis","journal":"Journal of Experimental Psychology: General","volume":"147","issue":"9","pages":"1349–1381","doi":"10.1037/xge0000462","pmid":"30148386","type":"Systematic review & meta-analysis","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["impulsiveness"],"finding":"Many experimental manipulations reduce delay discounting; episodic future thinking is well suited to therapy, and learning-based methods produced the largest and longest-lasting effects.","simple":"Impulsive choice can be reduced with practice — future thinking is one practical tool.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"bowen2014","authors":["Bowen S","Witkiewitz K","Clifasefi SL","Grow J","Chawla N","Hsu SH","Carroll HA","Harrop E","Collins SE","Lustyk MK","Larimer ME"],"year":2014,"title":"Relative Efficacy of Mindfulness-Based Relapse Prevention, Standard Relapse Prevention, and Treatment as Usual for Substance Use Disorders","journal":"JAMA Psychiatry","volume":"71","issue":"5","pages":"547","doi":"10.1001/jamapsychiatry.2013.4546","pmid":"24647726","type":"Randomized controlled trial","n":"286 adults in addiction aftercare","effect":"","areas":["impulsiveness"],"finding":"At 12 months, mindfulness-based relapse prevention reduced drug use and heavy drinking more than standard relapse prevention and usual care.","simple":"Mindfulness skills for cravings gave the longest-lasting protection against relapse.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"rizvi2014","authors":["Rizvi SL","Ritschel LA"],"year":2014,"title":"Mastering the Art of Chain Analysis in Dialectical Behavior Therapy","journal":"Cognitive and Behavioral Practice","volume":"21","issue":"3","pages":"335–349","doi":"10.1016/j.cbpra.2013.09.002","pmid":"","type":"Clinical method paper","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["impulsiveness"],"finding":"How to conduct a DBT chain analysis: identify vulnerabilities, the prompting event, each link (thoughts, feelings, sensations, actions), the problem behaviour and its consequences, then find solutions for each link.","simple":"Map the chain of events that led to an impulsive act, then plan where to break it.","verified":"Crossref"},
  {"id":"linehan2014","authors":["Linehan MM"],"year":2014,"title":"DBT Skills Training Manual (2nd ed.)","publisher":"Guilford Press","type":"Treatment manual","areas":["impulsiveness"],"finding":"Mindfulness, distress tolerance (e.g., STOP, TIPP, pros and cons), emotion regulation (e.g., reducing vulnerability with sleep, food, exercise) and interpersonal effectiveness skills.","simple":"Practical skills for surviving intense emotions without making things worse.","journal":"","volume":"","issue":"","pages":"","doi":"","pmid":"","n":"—","effect":"","verified":"Bibliographic record"},
  {"id":"daniel2013","authors":["Daniel TO","Stanton CM","Epstein LH"],"year":2013,"title":"The future is now: Reducing impulsivity and energy intake using episodic future thinking","journal":"Psychological Science","volume":"24","issue":"11","pages":"2339–2342","doi":"10.1177/0956797613488780","pmid":"24022653","type":"Randomized experiment","n":"Overweight and obese adults","effect":"","areas":["impulsiveness"],"finding":"Episodic future thinking reduced delay discounting and energy (food) intake compared with a control imagery task.","simple":"Picturing upcoming events reduced impulsive eating.","verified":"Crossref + OpenAlex abstract"},
  {"id":"anderson2011","authors":["Anderson C","Platten CR"],"year":2011,"title":"Sleep deprivation lowers inhibition and enhances impulsivity to negative stimuli","journal":"Behavioural Brain Research","volume":"217","issue":"2","pages":"463–466","doi":"10.1016/j.bbr.2010.09.020","pmid":"20888369","type":"Experiment","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["impulsiveness"],"finding":"One night of sleep deprivation increased impulsive responding to negative stimuli (more failures to inhibit and faster incorrect responses).","simple":"Being short on sleep makes you more impulsive.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"peters2010","authors":["Peters J","Büchel C"],"year":2010,"title":"Episodic Future Thinking Reduces Reward Delay Discounting through an Enhancement of Prefrontal-Mediotemporal Interactions","journal":"Neuron","volume":"66","issue":"1","pages":"138–148","doi":"10.1016/j.neuron.2010.03.026","pmid":"20399735","type":"fMRI experiment","n":"2 experiments","effect":"","areas":["impulsiveness","self-discipline"],"finding":"Vividly imagining specific personal future events reduced delay discounting (impulsive choice), via interactions between prefrontal and memory regions.","simple":"Imagining your future in detail makes it easier to choose long-term rewards.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"safren2010","authors":["Safren SA","Sprich S","Mimiaga MJ","Surman C","Knouse L","Groves M","Otto MW"],"year":2010,"title":"Cognitive Behavioral Therapy vs Relaxation With Educational Support for Medication-Treated Adults With ADHD and Persistent Symptoms","journal":"JAMA","volume":"304","issue":"8","pages":"875","doi":"10.1001/jama.2010.1192","pmid":"20736471","type":"Randomized controlled trial","n":"86 adults with ADHD","effect":"","areas":["impulsiveness","productivity"],"finding":"For medicated adults with persistent ADHD symptoms, CBT improved symptoms more than relaxation with educational support, and gains were maintained at 6 and 12 months.","simple":"CBT skills help adults with ADHD manage impulsivity and organisation.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"bowen2009","authors":["Bowen S","Marlatt A"],"year":2009,"title":"Surfing the urge: Brief mindfulness-based intervention for college student smokers","journal":"Psychology of Addictive Behaviors","volume":"23","issue":"4","pages":"666–671","doi":"10.1037/a0017127","pmid":"20025372","type":"Randomized experiment","n":"123 student smokers","effect":"","areas":["impulsiveness"],"finding":"Brief urge-surfing instructions did not reduce urge intensity, but participants smoked significantly fewer cigarettes over the next 7 days — mindfulness changed the response to urges.","simple":"You can feel a strong urge and still not act on it — riding the wave works.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"simon2009","authors":["Simon V","Czobor P","Bálint S","Mészáros Á","Bitter I"],"year":2009,"title":"Prevalence and correlates of adult attention-deficit hyperactivity disorder: meta-analysis","journal":"British Journal of Psychiatry","volume":"194","issue":"3","pages":"204–211","doi":"10.1192/bjp.bp.107.048827","pmid":"19252145","type":"Meta-analysis","n":"—","effect":"2.5% adult prevalence","areas":["impulsiveness"],"finding":"The pooled prevalence of adult ADHD was 2.5% (95% CI 2.1–3.1), declining with age.","simple":"About 1 in 40 adults has ADHD, a common cause of lifelong impulsivity.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"gawrilow2008","authors":["Gawrilow C","Gollwitzer PM"],"year":2008,"title":"Implementation Intentions Facilitate Response Inhibition in Children with ADHD","journal":"Cognitive Therapy and Research","volume":"32","issue":"2","pages":"261–280","doi":"10.1007/s10608-007-9150-1","pmid":"","type":"Experiments","n":"Children with ADHD","effect":"","areas":["impulsiveness"],"finding":"Implementation intentions (if–then plans) improved response inhibition in children with ADHD.","simple":"If–then plans help even when impulse control is especially hard.","verified":"Crossref"},
  {"id":"linehan2006","authors":["Linehan MM","Comtois KA","Murray AM","Brown MZ","Gallop RJ","Heard HL","Korslund KE","Tutek DA","Reynolds SK","Lindenboim N"],"year":2006,"title":"Two-Year Randomized Controlled Trial and Follow-up of Dialectical Behavior Therapy vs Therapy by Experts for Suicidal Behaviors and Borderline Personality Disorder","journal":"Archives of General Psychiatry","volume":"63","issue":"7","pages":"757","doi":"10.1001/archpsyc.63.7.757","pmid":"16818865","type":"Randomized controlled trial","n":"101 women","effect":"Half as likely to attempt suicide","areas":["impulsiveness"],"finding":"Dialectical Behaviour Therapy halved suicide attempts compared with treatment by community experts and reduced hospitalisations, emergency visits and dropout.","simple":"DBT skills dramatically reduced the most dangerous impulsive behaviours.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"kavanagh2005","authors":["Kavanagh DJ","Andrade J","May J"],"year":2005,"title":"Imaginary Relish and Exquisite Torture: The Elaborated Intrusion Theory of Desire","journal":"Psychological Review","volume":"112","issue":"2","pages":"446–467","doi":"10.1037/0033-295x.112.2.446","pmid":"15783293","type":"Theory","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["impulsiveness","self-discipline"],"finding":"Elaborated intrusion theory: cravings begin as intrusive thoughts and grow through vivid sensory imagery; competing tasks that occupy imagery and working memory can reduce craving.","simple":"Cravings grow when you picture the treat — redirecting your imagination shrinks them.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"delvecchio2004","authors":["Del Vecchio T","O'Leary K"],"year":2004,"title":"Effectiveness of anger treatments for specific anger problems: A meta-analytic review","journal":"Clinical Psychology Review","volume":"24","issue":"1","pages":"15–34","doi":"10.1016/j.cpr.2003.09.006","pmid":"14992805","type":"Meta-analysis","n":"Adults with demonstrable anger problems","effect":"","areas":["impulsiveness"],"finding":"Anger treatments produced medium-to-large effects; cognitive therapies were supported for trait anger, anger suppression and driving anger, and relaxation for state anger.","simple":"Both changing angry thoughts and learning to relax help with anger.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"moeller2001","authors":["Moeller FG","Barratt ES","Dougherty DM","Schmitz JM","Swann AC"],"year":2001,"title":"Psychiatric Aspects of Impulsivity","journal":"American Journal of Psychiatry","volume":"158","issue":"11","pages":"1783–1793","doi":"10.1176/appi.ajp.158.11.1783","pmid":"11691682","type":"Review","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["impulsiveness"],"finding":"Defines impulsivity as a predisposition toward rapid, unplanned reactions to internal or external stimuli without regard to the negative consequences for oneself or others.","simple":"Impulsivity means acting fast, without planning or thinking about consequences.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"whiteside2001","authors":["Whiteside SP","Lynam DR"],"year":2001,"title":"The Five Factor Model and impulsivity: using a structural model of personality to understand impulsivity","journal":"Personality and Individual Differences","volume":"30","issue":"4","pages":"669–689","doi":"10.1016/s0191-8869(00)00064-7","pmid":"","type":"Measure development","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["impulsiveness"],"finding":"The UPPS model separates urgency (acting rashly when upset), lack of premeditation, lack of perseverance and sensation seeking as distinct routes to impulsive behaviour.","simple":"Some people act impulsively when upset, others when bored or excited — knowing which helps.","verified":"Crossref"},
  {"id":"beck1998","authors":["Beck R","Fernandez E"],"year":1998,"title":"Cognitive-Behavioral Therapy in the Treatment of Anger: A Meta-Analysis","journal":"Cognitive Therapy and Research","volume":"22","issue":"1","pages":"63–74","doi":"10.1023/a:1018763902991","pmid":"","type":"Meta-analysis","n":"50 studies, 1,640 participants","effect":"d ≈ 0.70","areas":["impulsiveness"],"finding":"CBT for anger produced a medium-to-large effect (about 0.70): the average treated person did better than about 76% of untreated people.","simple":"CBT helps most people manage anger better.","verified":"Crossref"},
  {"id":"patton1995","authors":["Patton JH","Stanford MS","Barratt ES"],"year":1995,"title":"Factor structure of the barratt impulsiveness scale","journal":"Journal of Clinical Psychology","volume":"51","issue":"6","pages":"768–774","doi":"10.1002/1097-4679(199511)51:6<768::aid-jclp2270510607>3.0.co;2-1","pmid":"8778124","type":"Measure development","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["impulsiveness"],"finding":"The Barratt Impulsiveness Scale (BIS-11) identified attentional, motor and non-planning facets of impulsiveness.","simple":"Impulsiveness has different flavours: attention, acting without thinking and not planning ahead.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"roth2008","authors":["Roth AD","Pilling S"],"year":2008,"title":"Using an Evidence-Based Methodology to Identify the Competences Required to Deliver Effective Cognitive and Behavioural Therapy for Depression and Anxiety Disorders","journal":"Behavioural and Cognitive Psychotherapy","volume":"36","issue":"2","pages":"129–147","doi":"10.1017/s1352465808004141","pmid":"","type":"Competence framework","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["general"],"finding":"Describes how the competences needed to deliver effective CBT for depression and anxiety were identified from the treatments used in successful trials, for England's IAPT programme. The framework groups them into five domains: generic therapeutic competences, basic CBT competences, specific CBT techniques, problem-specific competences and metacompetences.","simple":"Psychologists' own definition of what doing CBT well involves.","verified":"Crossref + OpenAlex abstract"},
  {"id":"muse2013","authors":["Muse K","McManus F"],"year":2013,"title":"A systematic review of methods for assessing competence in cognitive–behavioural therapy","journal":"Clinical Psychology Review","volume":"33","issue":"3","pages":"484–499","doi":"10.1016/j.cpr.2013.01.010","pmid":"23454222","type":"Systematic review","n":"64 articles","effect":"","areas":["general"],"finding":"Reviewed ten methods of assessing CBT therapist competence. Session-rating scales such as the Cognitive Therapy Scale (and its revision, the CTS-R) rate agenda setting, feedback, collaboration, pacing, guided discovery, conceptualisation, key cognitions, change methods, behavioural techniques and homework.","simple":"How experts judge whether a CBT session was done well.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"blackburn2001","authors":["Blackburn IM","James IA","Milne DL","Baker C","Standart S","Garland A","Reichelt FK"],"year":2001,"title":"The Revised Cognitive Therapy Scale (CTS-R): psychometric properties","journal":"Behavioural and Cognitive Psychotherapy","volume":"29","issue":"4","pages":"431–446","doi":"10.1017/s1352465801004040","pmid":"","type":"Measure development","n":"102 therapy recordings","effect":"","areas":["general"],"finding":"The CTS-R, used to rate the competence of cognitive therapy sessions, showed high internal consistency and adequate inter-rater reliability, and ratings rose as trainee therapists gained experience.","simple":"The checklist psychologists use to rate a CBT session.","verified":"Crossref + OpenAlex abstract"},
  {"id":"cuijpers2010gsh","authors":["Cuijpers P","Donker T","van Straten A","Li J","Andersson G"],"year":2010,"title":"Is guided self-help as effective as face-to-face psychotherapy for depression and anxiety disorders? A systematic review and meta-analysis of comparative outcome studies","journal":"Psychological Medicine","volume":"40","issue":"12","pages":"1943–1957","doi":"10.1017/s0033291710000772","pmid":"20406528","type":"Meta-analysis","n":"21 RCTs, 810 participants","effect":"d = −0.02 vs face-to-face","areas":["general","depression","anxiety"],"finding":"In trials that compared them directly, guided self-help and face-to-face psychotherapy for depression and anxiety had comparable effects (d = −0.02 at post-test), with no significant difference at follow-up of up to a year and similar drop-out rates.","simple":"Self-help with some support from a professional worked about as well as face-to-face therapy.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"carlbring2018","authors":["Carlbring P","Andersson G","Cuijpers P","Riper H","Hedman-Lagerlöf E"],"year":2018,"title":"Internet-based vs. face-to-face cognitive behavior therapy for psychiatric and somatic disorders: an updated systematic review and meta-analysis","journal":"Cognitive Behaviour Therapy","volume":"47","issue":"1","pages":"1–18","doi":"10.1080/16506073.2017.1401115","pmid":"29215315","type":"Meta-analysis","n":"20 RCTs, 1,418 participants","effect":"g = 0.05 (equivalent)","areas":["general"],"finding":"Guided internet CBT and face-to-face CBT, compared within the same trials, produced equivalent overall effects (g = 0.05), although few trials exist for each individual condition.","simple":"Guided online CBT and in-person CBT worked equally well in head-to-head trials.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"karyotaki2021","authors":["Karyotaki E","Efthimiou O","Miguel C","Maas genannt Bermpohl F","Furukawa TA","Cuijpers P","Riper H","Patel V","Mira A","Gemmil AW"],"year":2021,"title":"Internet-Based Cognitive Behavioral Therapy for Depression: A Systematic Review and Individual Patient Data Network Meta-analysis","journal":"JAMA Psychiatry","volume":"78","issue":"4","pages":"361–371","doi":"10.1001/jamapsychiatry.2020.4364","pmid":"33471111","type":"Individual-participant network meta-analysis","n":"39 RCTs, 9,751 participants","effect":"Guided vs unguided: −0.8 PHQ-9 points","areas":["depression","general"],"finding":"Both guided and unguided internet CBT beat control conditions in the short and long term. Guided iCBT was more effective at post-treatment (0.8 PHQ-9 points), with the benefit concentrated in people starting with PHQ-9 above 9; for subthreshold symptoms (PHQ-9 5–9), unguided iCBT worked about as well.","simple":"Self-guided online CBT helps. With moderate or worse symptoms, adding human support helps more.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"dejong2021","authors":["de Jong K","Conijn JM","Gallagher RAV","Reshetnikova AS","Heij M","Lutz MC"],"year":2021,"title":"Using progress feedback to improve outcomes and reduce drop-out, treatment duration, and deterioration: A multilevel meta-analysis","journal":"Clinical Psychology Review","volume":"85","issue":"","pages":"102002","doi":"10.1016/j.cpr.2021.102002","pmid":"33721605","type":"Multilevel meta-analysis","n":"58 studies, 21,699 patients","effect":"d = 0.15 (not-on-track d = 0.17)","areas":["general"],"finding":"Feeding progress measures back during psychological treatment had a small, significant effect on symptom reduction (d = 0.15), including for people who were not on track (d = 0.17), and a small favourable effect on drop-out.","simple":"Tracking progress — and acting when you're not improving — makes therapy work a little better.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"tryon2018","authors":["Tryon GS","Birch SE","Verkuilen J"],"year":2018,"title":"Meta-analyses of the relation of goal consensus and collaboration to psychotherapy outcome","journal":"Psychotherapy","volume":"55","issue":"4","pages":"372–383","doi":"10.1037/pst0000170","pmid":"30335451","type":"Meta-analysis","n":"54 studies, 7,278 patients","effect":"r = .24 (goal consensus); r = .29 (collaboration)","areas":["general"],"finding":"Agreeing on therapy goals (r = .24, d = 0.49) and working collaboratively (r = .29, d = 0.61) were both linked with better psychotherapy outcomes — medium effects.","simple":"Clear, agreed goals and working together go with better results in therapy.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"braun2015","authors":["Braun JD","Strunk DR","Sasso KE","Cooper AA"],"year":2015,"title":"Therapist use of Socratic questioning predicts session-to-session symptom change in cognitive therapy for depression","journal":"Behaviour Research and Therapy","volume":"70","issue":"","pages":"32–37","doi":"10.1016/j.brat.2015.05.004","pmid":"25965026","type":"Process study","n":"55 adults with depression","effect":"1 SD more questioning → 1.51 BDI-II points lower next session","areas":["depression","general"],"finding":"More Socratic questioning by the therapist in early sessions of cognitive therapy for depression predicted greater symptom improvement by the next session, independent of the therapeutic alliance.","simple":"Being asked good questions — rather than told the answers — helps people rethink.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"biesheuvel2015","authors":["Biesheuvel-Leliefeld KEM","Kok GD","Bockting CLH","Cuijpers P","Hollon SD","van Marwijk HWJ","Smit F"],"year":2015,"title":"Effectiveness of psychological interventions in preventing recurrence of depressive disorder: meta-analysis and meta-regression","journal":"Journal of Affective Disorders","volume":"174","issue":"","pages":"400–410","doi":"10.1016/j.jad.2014.12.016","pmid":"25553400","type":"Meta-analysis","n":"25 RCTs","effect":"RR 0.64 vs usual care (NNT 5)","areas":["depression","general"],"finding":"Preventive psychological interventions reduced the risk of depressive relapse or recurrence compared with usual care (RR 0.64, NNT 5) and compared with antidepressants (RR 0.83, NNT 13).","simple":"Learning how to stay well cuts the chance of depression coming back.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"gellatly2007","authors":["Gellatly J","Bower P","Hennessy S","Richards D","Gilbody S","Lovell K"],"year":2007,"title":"What makes self-help interventions effective in the management of depressive symptoms? Meta-analysis and meta-regression","journal":"Psychological Medicine","volume":"37","issue":"9","pages":"1217–1228","doi":"10.1017/s0033291707000062","pmid":"17306044","type":"Meta-analysis","n":"34 studies","effect":"","areas":["depression","general"],"finding":"Of the factors examined, only guidance — contact with a therapist alongside the self-help materials — remained a significant predictor of better outcomes in the multivariate analysis.","simple":"Self-help works better when someone supports you through it.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"mundt2002","authors":["Mundt JC","Marks IM","Shear MK","Greist JH"],"year":2002,"title":"The Work and Social Adjustment Scale: a simple measure of impairment in functioning","journal":"British Journal of Psychiatry","volume":"180","issue":"5","pages":"461–464","doi":"10.1192/bjp.180.5.461","pmid":"11983645","type":"Measure development","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["general"],"finding":"The WSAS, a five-item measure of how problems affect work, home, social and private leisure and relationships, was reliable (alpha 0.70–0.94; test–retest 0.73), valid and sensitive to treatment-related change.","simple":"How much problems get in the way of everyday life is worth measuring, not just symptoms.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"linardon2019","authors":["Linardon J","Cuijpers P","Carlbring P","Messer M","Fuller-Tyszkiewicz M"],"year":2019,"title":"The efficacy of app-supported smartphone interventions for mental health problems: a meta-analysis of randomized controlled trials","journal":"World Psychiatry","volume":"18","issue":"3","pages":"325–336","doi":"10.1002/wps.20673","pmid":"31496095","type":"Meta-analysis","n":"66 RCTs","effect":"Depression g = 0.28; anxiety g = 0.30","areas":["general"],"finding":"Smartphone interventions improved depressive (g = 0.28) and generalised anxiety (g = 0.30) symptoms; CBT-based apps that offered professional guidance and reminders to engage produced larger effects.","simple":"CBT apps help — more so with reminders and a professional's support.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"barlow2017","authors":["Barlow DH","Farchione TJ","Bullis JR","Gallagher MW","Murray-Latin H","Sauer-Zavala S","Bentley KH","Thompson-Hollands J","Conklin LR","Boswell JF"],"year":2017,"title":"The Unified Protocol for Transdiagnostic Treatment of Emotional Disorders Compared With Diagnosis-Specific Protocols for Anxiety Disorders: A Randomized Clinical Trial","journal":"JAMA Psychiatry","volume":"74","issue":"9","pages":"875–884","doi":"10.1001/jamapsychiatry.2017.2164","pmid":"28768327","type":"Randomized controlled trial","n":"223 patients","effect":"UP d = −0.93; disorder-specific d = −1.08 (equivalent)","areas":["anxiety","general"],"finding":"A single transdiagnostic CBT protocol, targeting processes shared across emotional disorders, produced symptom reductions equivalent to disorder-specific CBT protocols, with less drop-out (OR 3.11 for completing treatment).","simple":"The same core CBT processes help across different anxiety problems.","verified":"Crossref + PubMed abstract"},
  {"id":"kuyken2009","authors":["Kuyken W","Padesky CA","Dudley R"],"year":2009,"title":"Collaborative Case Conceptualization: Working Effectively with Clients in Cognitive-Behavioral Therapy","journal":"","volume":"","issue":"","pages":"","doi":"","pmid":"","type":"Clinical guide","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["general"],"finding":"Presents case conceptualisation as a collaborative, evolving map built in levels — describing the problem in CBT terms, explaining what triggers and maintains it, then (if needed) how it developed — guided by collaborative empiricism and built around the person's strengths. ISBN 978-1-60623-072-5.","simple":"How CBT therapists and clients build a shared map of the problem, including strengths.","verified":"Bibliographic record","publisher":"Guilford Press"},
  {"id":"beck2020","authors":["Beck JS"],"year":2020,"title":"Cognitive Behavior Therapy: Basics and Beyond (3rd ed.)","journal":"","volume":"","issue":"","pages":"","doi":"","pmid":"","type":"Clinical textbook","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["general"],"finding":"The standard CBT textbook. Sessions follow a consistent structure: a mood check, setting the agenda, reviewing the previous action plan (homework), working on a problem while teaching skills, setting a new action plan, and a summary with feedback. ISBN 978-1-4625-4419-6.","simple":"The textbook that describes how a CBT session is structured.","verified":"Bibliographic record","publisher":"Guilford Press"},
  {"id":"apa2019","authors":["American Psychological Association"],"year":2019,"title":"Clinical Practice Guideline for the Treatment of Depression Across Three Age Cohorts","journal":"","volume":"","issue":"","pages":"","doi":"","pmid":"","type":"Clinical guideline","n":"—","effect":"","areas":["depression","general"],"finding":"For adults, recommends psychotherapy or second-generation antidepressants as initial treatment, with cognitive-behavioural therapy, cognitive therapy and behavioural therapy among the recommended psychotherapies. An update of the guideline was commissioned in 2025.","simple":"The American Psychological Association recommends CBT for depression.","verified":"Official source","publisher":"American Psychological Association","url":"https://www.apa.org/depression-guideline/adults"}
